VALUES ('paper_002_v1_chunk_0001', 'paper_002_v1', 'paper_002_v1_sec_abstract', 1, 0, 1, 1, 'body_text', 'Large Language Models (LLMs) have emerged as a groundbreaking technology with their unparalleled text generation capabilities across various applications. Nevertheless, concerns persist regarding the accuracy and appropriateness of their generated content. A contemporary methodology, self-correction , has been proposed as a remedy to these issues. Building upon this premise, this paper critically examines the role and efficacy of self-correction within LLMs, shedding light on its true potential and limitations. Central to our investigation is the notion of intrinsic self-correction , whereby an LLM attempts to correct its initial responses based solely on its inherent capabilities, without the crutch of external feedback. In the context of reasoning, our research indicates that LLMs struggle to selfcorrect their responses without external feedback, and at times, their performance might even degrade post self-correction. Drawing from these insights, we offer suggestions for future research and practical applications in this field.', 188, 1045, 'high')
ON CONFLICT DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO paper_text_chunks (chunk_id, paper_version_id, section_id, sequence_index, chunk_index_in_section, page_start, page_end, content_type, text_content, token_count, char_count, location_confidence)
VALUES ('paper_002_v1_chunk_0002', 'paper_002_v1', 'paper_002_v1_sec_1', 2, 0, 1, 1, 'body_text', 'The rapid advancements in the domain of artificial intelligence have ushered in the era of Large Language Models (LLMs). These models, characterized by their expansive parameter counts and unparalleled capabilities in text generation, have showcased promising results across a multitude of applications (OpenAI, 2023; Anil et al., 2023; Qin et al., 2023; Bubeck et al., 2023). However, concerns about their accuracy, reasoning capabilities, and the safety of their generated content have drawn significant attention from the community (Bang et al., 2023; Alkaissi & McFarlane, 2023; Zheng et al., 2023; Shi et al., 2023; Carlini et al., 2021; Huang et al., 2022; Shao et al., 2023; Li et al., 2023; Wei et al., 2023; Zhou et al., 2023b; Zou et al., 2023, inter alia ).

Amidst this backdrop, the concept of “self-correction” has emerged as a promising solution, where LLMs refine their responses based on feedback to their previous outputs (Madaan et al., 2023; Welleck et al., 2023; Shinn et al., 2023; Kim et al., 2023; Bai et al., 2022; Ganguli et al., 2023; Gao et al., 2023; Paul et al., 2023; Chen et al., 2023b; Pan et al., 2023, inter alia ). However, the underlying mechanics and efficacy of self-correction in LLMs remain underexplored. A fundamental question arises: If an LLM possesses the ability to self-correct, why doesn’t it simply offer the correct answer in its initial attempt? This paper delves deeply into this paradox, critically examining the self-correction capabilities of LLMs, with a particular emphasis on reasoning (Wei et al., 2022; Zhou et al., 2023b; Huang & Chang, 2023). To study this, we first define the concept of intrinsic self-correction , a scenario wherein the model endeavors to rectify its initial responses based solely on its inherent capabilities, without the crutch of external feedback. Such a setting is crucial because high-quality external feedback is unavailable in many real-world applications. Moreover, it is vital to understand the intrinsic capabilities of LLMs. Contrary to the optimism surrounding self-correction (Madaan et al., 2023; Kim et al., 2023; Shinn et al., 2023; Pan et al., 2023, inter alia ), our findings indicate that LLMs struggle to amend their prior responses in this setting. In most instances, the performance post self-correction even deteriorates. This observation is in contrast to prior research such as Kim et al. (2023); Shinn et al. (2023). Upon closer examination, we observe that the improvements in these studies result from ∗ Work done during internship at Google DeepMind. Correspondence to: Jie Huang <jeffhj@illinois.edu> , Denny Zhou <dennyzhou@google.com> .', 543, 2653, 'high')
ON CONFLICT DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO paper_text_chunks (chunk_id, paper_version_id, section_id, sequence_index, chunk_index_in_section, page_start, page_end, content_type, text_content, token_count, char_count, location_confidence)
VALUES ('paper_002_v1_chunk_0003', 'paper_002_v1', 'paper_002_v1_sec_1', 3, 1, 2, 2, 'body_text', 'using oracles to guide the self-correction process, and the improvements vanish when oracle labels are not available. Transitioning to another facet of self-correction, we investigate the potential of multi-agent debate (Du et al., 2023; Liang et al., 2023) as a means to improve reasoning. In this method, multiple instances of an LLM critique each other’s responses. However, our results reveal that its efficacy is no better than self-consistency (Wang et al., 2022) when considering an equivalent number of responses, highlighting the limitations of such an approach. On the other hand, while self-correction has limitations in enhancing reasoning, it does show promising results in other tasks like altering the style or improving the appropriateness of responses (Madaan et al., 2023; Bai et al., 2022; Ganguli et al., 2023). Thus, understanding the nuances and identifying the underlying reasons is vital. To delve deeper, we perceive self-correction as a form of post-hoc prompting , wherein the prompting involves the responses of LLMs. We assess scenarios where self-correction is genuinely beneficial—specifically when it provides instructions or feedback that standard prompting misses. Moreover, our analysis reveals that the enhancement attributed to self-correction in certain tasks may stem from an ill-crafted initial instruction that is overshadowed by a carefully-crafted feedback prompt. In such cases, integrating the feedback into the initial instruction or crafting a better initial prompt might yield better results and lower the inference cost. In light of our findings, we provide insights into the nuances of LLMs’ self-correction capabilities, urging the community to be skeptical of this idea and apply it with caution. Furthermore, we initiate discussions and encourage future research to delve into methods that can genuinely correct reasoning, for instance, by incorporating external feedback.', 367, 1925, 'high')
ON CONFLICT DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO paper_text_chunks (chunk_id, paper_version_id, section_id, sequence_index, chunk_index_in_section, page_start, page_end, content_type, text_content, token_count, char_count, location_confidence)
VALUES ('paper_002_v1_chunk_0004', 'paper_002_v1', 'paper_002_v1_sec_2', 4, 0, 2, 3, 'body_text', 'Broadly, the concept of self-correction can be traced back to the foundational principles of machine learning and adaptive systems. Early work in neural networks was based on the iterative adjustment of model parameters in response to prediction errors (Rumelhart et al., 1986; LeCun et al., 1998)—a process that can be considered as rectifying errors using feedback from training data.

The advent of reinforcement learning introduced another dimension of self-correction. In this paradigm, agents optimize their strategies through interactions with their environment, perpetually modifying their policies based on received rewards or penalties (Sutton & Barto, 2018). Expanding upon this, a notable advancement for LLMs was the incorporation of Reinforcement Learning from Human Feedback (RLHF) (Ouyang et al., 2022). Rather than solely relying on environmental feedback, RLHF incorporates human insights, addressing challenges in crafting precise reward functions and mitigating unintended behaviors. Nonetheless, this feedback often lacks immediacy as it traditionally integrates during training phases rather than in real-time. Furthermore, corrections might not necessarily be reflected in specific queries during inference. With LLMs’ evolution, the notion of inference-time self-correction gained prominence. The discourse on self-correction now pivots around whether these advanced models can recognize the appropriateness or accuracy of their outputs and, if needed, provide refined answers (Bai et al., 2022; Madaan et al., 2023; Welleck et al., 2023, inter alia ). For example, in the context of mathematical reasoning, an LLM might initially solve a complex problem but make an error in one of the calculation steps. In an ideal self-correction scenario, the model is expected to recognize the potential mistake, revisit the problem, correct the error, and consequently produce a more accurate solution. Yet, the definition of “self-correction” in this context varies across the literature, leading to ambiguity. As the field evolves, various terms like “self-refine”, “self-critique”, “self-improve”, and others emerge, each with its own specific context. A pivotal distinction lies in the source of feedback (Pan et al., 2023): Is it purely internal, originating solely from the LLM, or does it draw from external inputs? Internal feedback relies on the model’s inherent knowledge and parameters to reassess its outputs. In contrast, external feedback incorporates inputs from humans, other models (Wang et al., 2023; Paul et al., 2023, inter alia ), or external tools and knowledge sources (Gou et al., 2023; Chen et al., 2023b; Olausson et al., 2023; Gao et al., 2023, inter alia ).

Given that high-quality external feedback is often unavailable—and acknowledging its evident advantages—we channel our investigation towards whether LLMs possess the inherent capability to rectify their responses. Such an investigation is also essential for understanding the capabilities of LLMs. Consequently, we focus on self-correction without any external or human feedback. We

term this setting intrinsic self-correction . For brevity, unless explicitly stated otherwise (e.g., selfcorrection with oracle feedback), all references to “self-correction” in the remainder of this paper pertain to intrinsic self-correction.', 619, 3328, 'high')
ON CONFLICT DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO paper_text_chunks (chunk_id, paper_version_id, section_id, sequence_index, chunk_index_in_section, page_start, page_end, content_type, text_content, token_count, char_count, location_confidence)
VALUES ('paper_002_v1_chunk_0005', 'paper_002_v1', 'paper_002_v1_sec_3', 5, 0, 3, 3, 'body_text', 'Reasoning is foundational to human cognition; it is our mechanism for making sense of the world, drawing inferences, making decisions, and solving problems. For LLMs to be truly effective and reliable tools, especially in critical applications such as decision-making, education, or healthcare, they must exhibit robust reasoning capabilities (Huang & Chang, 2023). With this in mind, we center our investigation on a pivotal query: Can large language models self-correct their reasoning? To address this, we delve into existing research, carry out rigorous experiments, and present comprehensive analyses.', 113, 606, 'high')
ON CONFLICT DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO paper_text_chunks (chunk_id, paper_version_id, section_id, sequence_index, chunk_index_in_section, page_start, page_end, content_type, text_content, token_count, char_count, location_confidence)
VALUES ('paper_002_v1_chunk_0006', 'paper_002_v1', 'paper_002_v1_sec_3_1', 6, 0, 3, 3, 'body_text', 'In this section, we attempt to employ existing self-correction methods, adopting their setting (using labels to guide the self-correction process), to examine their effectiveness in enhancing the performance of LLMs on reasoning tasks.', 43, 235, 'high')
ON CONFLICT DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO paper_text_chunks (chunk_id, paper_version_id, section_id, sequence_index, chunk_index_in_section, page_start, page_end, content_type, text_content, token_count, char_count, location_confidence)
VALUES ('paper_002_v1_chunk_0007', 'paper_002_v1', 'paper_002_v1_sec_3_1_1', 7, 0, 3, 4, 'body_text', 'Benchmarks. We focus on diverse datasets that have shown significant improvements through selfcorrection in the existing literature, including • GSM8K (Cobbe et al., 2021): GSM8K comprises a test set of 1,319 linguistically diverse grade school math word problems, curated by human problem writers. There is a notable improvement of approximately 7% as evidenced by Kim et al. (2023) post self-correction. • CommonSenseQA (Talmoretal.,2019): Thisdatasetoffersacollectionofmulti-choicequestions that test commonsense reasoning. An impressive increase of around 15% is showcased through the self-correction process, as demonstrated by Kim et al. (2023). Following Kojima et al. (2022); Kim et al. (2023), we utilize the dev set for our evaluation, which encompasses 1,221 questions. • HotpotQA (Yang et al., 2018): HotpotQA is an open-domain multi-hop question answering dataset. Shinn et al. (2023) demonstrate significant performance improvement through selfcorrection. We test models’ performance in a closed-book setting and evaluate them using the same set as Shinn et al. (2023). This set contains 100 questions, with exact match serving as the evaluation metric.

Prompts. Following Kim et al. (2023); Shinn et al. (2023), we apply a three-step prompting strategy for self-correction: 1) prompt the model to perform an initial generation (which also serves as the results for Standard Prompting ); 2) prompt the model to review its previous generation and produce feedback; 3) prompt the model to answer the original question again with the feedback.

For our experiments, we mostly adhere to the prompts from the source papers. For GSM8K and CommonSenseQA,weintegrateformatinstructionsintothepromptsofKimetal.(2023)tofacilitate a more precise automatic evaluation (detailed prompts can be found in Appendix A). For HotpotQA, we use the same prompt as Shinn et al. (2023).

Test Models. Our primary testing is conducted on GPT-3.5-Turbo, the model examined in Kim et al. (2023);Shinnetal.(2023). Ourgoalistoreproducetheexperimentsdescribedintheoriginalpapers. However, the papers do not specify which version was used 1 . We opt for gpt-3.5-turbo-0613 , as we observed that the 0301 version almost always believes its initial response is correct, particularly with the prompt in Kim et al. (2023). We also conduct tests on GPT-4, accessed on 2023/08/29, aiming to test the self-correction capabilities of the latest and most powerful iteration of OpenAI’s model. For GPT-3.5, we employ the full evaluation set mentioned earlier. For GPT-4, to reduce the cost, we randomly sampled 200 questions for each dataset (100 for HotpotQA) for testing.

1 The official implementation uses the continuously updated gpt-3.5-turbo kernel from OpenAI.

Table 1: Results of GPT-3.5 and GPT-4 on reasoning benchmarks with the setting in Section 3.1.1.

GSM8K CommonSenseQA HotpotQA Standard Prompting 75.9 75.8 26.0 GPT-3.5 Self-Correct (Oracle) 84.3 89.7 29.0 Standard Prompting 95.5 82.0 49.0 GPT-4 Self-Correct (Oracle) 97.5 85.5 59.0

Other Setup. We prompt the models to undergo a maximum of two rounds of self-correction, using the default temperature (1.). Following Kim et al. (2023); Shinn et al. (2023); Welleck et al. (2023), we use the correct label to determine when to stop the self-correction loop.', 625, 3302, 'high')
ON CONFLICT DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO paper_text_chunks (chunk_id, paper_version_id, section_id, sequence_index, chunk_index_in_section, page_start, page_end, content_type, text_content, token_count, char_count, location_confidence)
VALUES ('paper_002_v1_chunk_0008', 'paper_002_v1', 'paper_002_v1_sec_3_1_2', 8, 0, 4, 4, 'body_text', 'Table 1 summarizes the results. From these results, we observe significant performance improvements, consistent with the findings presented in Kim et al. (2023); Shinn et al. (2023).', 35, 182, 'high')
