VALUES ('paper_005_v5_chunk_0011', 'paper_005_v5', 'paper_005_v5_sec_3_1', 11, 0, 6, 6, 'body_text', 'We first introduce a useful building block for our results that we call the layer-norm hash . The layer-norm hash is a mechanism that enables retrieval across different columns in the transformer based on query-keymatching of numerical values. Exact-match retrieval is trivial when the query q i and keys k ,...k are items in a finite set: just one-hot encode q and k and the inner product will 1 i i j be maximized when q and k match. But this does not work when the keys and values are counts i j produced by uniform attention, which many transformer algorithms use (Weiss et al., 2021), as the key q /i and query k /j have different denominators. The layer-norm hash helps by transforming i j q /i and k /j so hardattentionretrieves j s.t. q = k . Let layer norm ( x ) = x ′ , where x ′ = x x ¯ . i j i j k x ′ k − Definition 3 (Layer-norm hash) . For x,y R , let φ ( x,y ) , layer norm ( x,y, x, y ) . ∈ − − φ ( x,y ) is a unit vector in R 4 . A key feature is scale invariance, and, in particular, that φ ( x/i, 1 /i ) is invariant w.r.t. i in the sense that it is only a function of x , independentof i . Let φ , φ ( x, 1) . Then x we have the following properties, whose proof may be found in Appendix C. Lemma 1 (Scale invariance) . For any x R and i R , φ ( x/i, 1 /i ) = φ . > 0 x ∈ ∈ Lemma 2 (Equality check via layer-norm hash) . For any q,k R , φ φ = 1 if and only if q = k . q k ∈ · In other words, the inner product of these representations of two scalars q and k , even if computed at different positions i and j , respectively, allows us to check for the equality of q and k . We can look up key q /i in a sequence of keys k / 1 ,...,k / ( i 1) by attending with query φ ( q /i, 1 /i ) i 1 i − 1 i − at position i and key φ ( k /j, 1 /j ) at each j < i . By Lemmas 1 and 2 this averages the values at j all j such that q = k . The layer-norm hash can also be used to directly compare two values q ,k i j i j without removing the denominator by computing φ ( q , 1) and φ ( k , 1) . i j', 611, 2002, 'high')
