VALUES ('paper_005_v5_chunk_0012', 'paper_005_v5', 'paper_005_v5_sec_3_2', 12, 0, 6, 7, 'body_text', 'We canusethelayer-normhashtosimulatemodelsofcomputationlikeautomataorTuringmachines with intermediate-generation transformers. To warm up, we first show how to use the layer-norm hash to simulate an automaton (i.e., recognize a regular language) and then extend it in Section 3.3 to show how a transformer can simulate a Turing machine for a bounded number of steps. Theorem 1 (Regular language recognition) . For any regular language L . there is a decoder-only projected pre-norm transformer with strict causal saturated attention (with or without positional encodings) that, on input $ x , 7 , 8 checks whether x L with x + 1 decoding steps. ∈ | | Proof. Let A be a finite-state automaton recognizing L . We will simulate one step of A with one transformer decoding step (after first reading n input tokens). We refer to tokens with 0 indexing: $ is token 0, x is token 1, etc. At step i,n i 2 n , we will output a token q encoding the next 1 i − n ≤ ≤ state of A . After printing the final state q , we use one additional step to output 1 iff q F , the set n n ∈ of final states of A . At each token i > 0 , we compute 1 /i by attending uniformly over the strict left context with value [ x = $] . We show by induction that at step i n , we can output q . j i − n 1 ≥ Base Case: i = n . For i n , we output q . Crucially, at i = n , this becomes the next input. ≤ Inductive Case: i > n . We alreadyhavea sequenceof intermediatetokens q ,...,q . Our goal 0 i − n − 1 is to compute q = δ ( q ,σ ) , which first involves retrieving q and σ . q i − n i − n − 1 i − n i − n − 1 i − n i − n − 1 is the input to the current column of the transformer. We will use hard attention to retrieve the current input symbol σ . To do this, we attend uniformly over the prior decoding tokens and $, i − n with a value of 1 at $ and 0 elsewhere. At tokens i > n (i.e., decoding tokens), this yields 1 . i − n Recall that projected pre-norm can simulate multi-pre-norm (Proposition 1). We now leverage the multi-pre-norm architecture to pass two layer-norms to a feedforward network: φ I , φ (1 /i, 1) , φ D , φ (1 / ( i n ) , 1) . i i − 7 Theorem 1 goes through without strict causal masking (but Theorem 2 will require strict masking). 8 Theorems 1 and 2 both go through without $ as long as token j can compute value v = [ j = 0] . j

Let d , [ x Q ] , where Q is the set of states of A . Based on d , we select between φ I and φ D : i i i i i 1 ∈ φ , ReLU ( d ~ 1 + φ I ) + ReLU ( d ~ 1 ~ 1 + φ D ) . i i i i i − − We attend with query layer norm ( φ ) = φ , key layer norm ( φ ) = φ if d = 0 and ~ 0 otherwise, i i j j j and value σ if d = 0 and ~ 0 otherwise. By Lemma 2, at the current step i , the attention score is j j maximizedwhen j = i n ,thusretrieving σ . We nowhavethepreviousstate q andcurrent i − n i − n − 1 − token σ . We conclude by computing q = δ ( q ,σ ) with a feedforward network. i − n i − n i − n − 1 i − n', 874, 2919, 'high')
