INSERT INTO paper_text_chunks (chunk_id, paper_version_id, section_id, sequence_index, chunk_index_in_section, page_start, page_end, content_type, text_content, token_count, char_count, location_confidence)
VALUES ('paper_008_openreview_2024_02_11_chunk_0011', 'paper_008_openreview_2024_02_11', 'paper_008_openreview_2024_02_11_sec_4_1', 11, 0, 6, 6, 'body_text', 'Motivated by prior successful efforts to evaluate LLM-generated text via LLMs, such as AlpacaEval (Dubois et al., 2023), FActScore (Min et al., 2023), and G-Eval (Liu et al., 2023b), B OOOOK S CORE automatically measures the coherence of summaries generated by a book-length summarization system via few-shot prompting. B OOOOK S CORE is both source-free and referencefree (i.e., it does not require access to the input book or a reference summary), similar to the SNaC classifier built for fine-tuned summarizers by Goyal et al. (2022a). Specification: Assume we have a summary S consisting of sentences s ,s ,...,s . 9 We develop 1 2 n a few-shot error-identification prompt E that instructs the LLM to identify any instances of one of the eight specified error types in a given sentence s of the summary. Concretely, we iterate over i each sentence s in the summary, feeding the prompt E , full summary S , and target sentence s i i at each step. There are two acceptable outputs at each step: either (1) no error is found and the LLM outputs No confusion , or (2) an error(s) is identified and the LLM is asked to generate a corresponding question and associated error type. We include two full summaries with 42 sentencelevel annotations in the prompt as demonstrations. 10 The B OOOOK S CORE of a single summary S (Figure 2) is then computed as:

1 (cid:88) B OOOOK S CORE ( S ) = [ LLM ( E,S,s ) == No confusion ] (1) n i s i ∈ S', 339, 1436, 'high')
ON CONFLICT DO NOTHING;

