INSERT INTO paper_text_chunks (chunk_id, paper_version_id, section_id, sequence_index, chunk_index_in_section, page_start, page_end, content_type, text_content, token_count, char_count, location_confidence)
VALUES ('paper_013_openreview_2024_02_11_chunk_0025', 'paper_013_openreview_2024_02_11', 'paper_013_openreview_2024_02_11_sec_a_1', 25, 0, 15, 16, 'body_text', 'This subsection aims to provide an accesible and completely formal definition of what we would call throughout Sec.2 and 3 a diffeomorphism . Definition7 ( C k -Diffeomorphism) . Let ˜ beaclosedsubsetof R KM . Afunction f : R KM ↣ R N is said to be a C k -diffeomorphism around Z ˜ , denoted by f C k Diffeo( ˜ , R N ) , if Z ∈ Z i ) f is defined in an open neighbourhood R KM of ˜ , W ⊆ Z ii ) f is k -times continuously differentiable on , i.e. f C k ( ) , W ∈ W iii ) f is injective on ˜ , i.e. bijective between ˜ and f ( ˜ ) and Z Z Z iv ) for any z ˜ the derivative D f ( z ) Lin( R KM , R N ) is injective (or equivalently, of full ∈ Z ∈ column-rank). Remark. In the special case when a function h is a diffeomorphism around ˜ R KM , but also maps to R KM , for any z ˜ the derivative D h ( z ) is a bijective, hence invertible Z ⊆ linear transformation of R KM . Besides that, ∈ Z because of iii ), h is injective. Based on the inverse function theorem we may conclude that h is injective in an open neighbourhood ′ of ˜ and h − 1 is also k -times h ( W ′ ) W Z | continuously differentiable (where, of course, h ( ˜ ) h ( ′ ) ). Z ⊆ W Hence, we arrive to the more familiar definition of a diffeomorphism , i.e. h being bijective with both h and h − 1 being continuously differentiable. However, the latter definition cannot be applied in the more general case when the dimensionality of the co-domain is larger than that of the domain, since f − 1 cannot be differentiable on a lower dimensional submanifold f ( ˜ ) . | f ( Z ˜ ) Z

With some variations, the mathematical object with the properties listed in Def.7 is often called an embedding and serves as a generator or a parametrization of a lower dimensional submanifold of R N . This is closer to the interpretation we employ here. Remark. The literature on diffeomorphisms often defines them as smooth, i.e. infinitely many times differentiable functions with smooth inverses. In our results and proofs, twice differentiability will suffice.', 542, 2007, 'high')
