t, W = argmin Φ W Y 2 , (3) t t − 1 t − 1 F ∀ R ∥ − ∥ W ∈ D × S

where Φ = [ ϕ ( x ) ,...,ϕ ( x )] ⊤ R τ × D denotes non-linear features for observations accumuτ 1 τ lated until time step τ , Y = [ y ∈ ,..., y ] ⊤ R τ × S is the corresponding targets, and τ 1 τ F ∈ || · || denotes the Frobenius norm. We can expect our world model with the weights satisfying Equation 3 to have good regret, as the regret of FTL for online linear regression problems with quadratic loss is Regret ( W ) = (log T ) (Shalev-Shwartzetal.,2012;Gaillardetal.,2019). Asaconsequence, our T O online learned world model will be immune to forgetting, rendering it suitable for lifelong agents. In the following sections, we present how to attain a feature representation that is expressive enough for modeling complex transition dynamics (Section 3.2), as well as how its sparsity helps to achieve efficient incremental update (Section 3.3).', 316, 1170, 'high')
