INSERT INTO paper_text_chunks (chunk_id, paper_version_id, section_id, sequence_index, chunk_index_in_section, page_start, page_end, content_type, text_content, token_count, char_count, location_confidence)
VALUES ('paper_016_openreview_2024_02_11_chunk_0010', 'paper_016_openreview_2024_02_11', 'paper_016_openreview_2024_02_11_sec_3_2', 10, 0, 4, 5, 'body_text', 'We construct high-dimensional sparse features using random projection followed by soft binning. The input vector x R S + A is first projected into feature space σ : x Px , where P R d × ( S + A ) t ∈ → ∈ is a random projection matrix sampled from a multivariate Gaussian distribution so that the transformation approximately preserves similarity in the original space (Johnson & Lindenstrauss, 1984). Afterwards, each element of σ ( x ) is binned in a soft manner by locating its neighboring edges as t indices and computing the distances between all edges as values . We denote the binning operation as b : R d R D , where D d . Compared to naive binning which produces binary one-hot vec- → ≫ tors and loses precision, ours has greater discriminative power by generating multi-hot real-valued representations. We provide the following example to illustrate. Assume we are binning σ ( x ) = 1 . 7 into a 1 -d grid 1 t with edges [0 , 1 , 2 , 3] . The naive binning would generate a vector [0 , 1 , 0] to indicate the value falls into the central bin. In comparison, the soft binning first locates the neighbors 1 and 2 as indices, and computes distances to the neighbors as values, thus forming a vector [0 , 0 . 7 , 0 . 3 , 0] . Our soft binning can discriminate two inputs falling inside the same bin, such as σ ( x ) = 1 . 7 and σ ( x ) = 1 . 2 , 1 t 1 t while naive binning fails to do so. Altogether, our feature encoder ϕ = b σ resembles Locality Sensitive Hashing (LSH) with random ◦ projections (Charikar, 2002) but is more expressive thanks to soft binning. We term our feature representation as Lo cality s ensitive s parse e ncoding, hence Losse . We depict the overall Losse process in Figure 3.

We note that the raw output of b can be multi-dimensional before flattening (e.g. 2 dimensional in Figure 3), and we use ρ to de- ⨂ note its dimensionality. A ρ -dimensional grid N has λ evenly spaced bins along each axis, thus producing a highly sparse feature vector of length λ ρ per grid. Finally, we can stack κ grids with different random projection directions to get diverse features. Losse has guarx σ ( x ) φ ( x ) t t t anteed sparsity as stated in Remark 3.1. σ b Remark 3.1 (Sparsity guarantee of Losse) . Figure 3: Locality sensitive sparse encoding. σ ( · ) For any , and any input vecρ,λ,κ > 0 projects input vectors into a random feature space, and ∈ Z tor x , ϕ ( x ) outputs a vector whose number of b ( · ) softly bins σ ( x ) into multiple ρ -dimensional grids, t nonzero entries ϕ ( x ) satisfies ϕ ( x ) which are flattened and stacked into a high-dimensional ∥ ∥ 0 ∥ ∥ 0 ≤ κ 2 ρ , and the proportion of nonzero entries in sparse encoding ϕ ( x ) . t (cid:0) 2 (cid:1) ρ ϕ ( x ) is at most . λ

To give a concrete example, if we set ρ = 3 and λ = 10 , then ϕ ( x ) is at most 8 κ while the ∥ ∥ dimension of ϕ ( x ) can be as high as 1000 κ . The high-dimensional feature brings us more fitting capacity, while the bounded sparsity permits efficient model updates with constant overheads, as we will show in the following section.', 811, 3061, 'high')
