INSERT INTO paper_text_chunks (chunk_id, paper_version_id, section_id, sequence_index, chunk_index_in_section, page_start, page_end, content_type, text_content, token_count, char_count, location_confidence)
VALUES ('paper_016_openreview_2024_02_11_chunk_0012', 'paper_016_openreview_2024_02_11', 'paper_016_openreview_2024_02_11_sec_3_3', 12, 1, 6, 6, 'body_text', 'where , denotes the Frobenius inner product. Treating W as a constant since it is not affected ⟨· ·⟩ F s by current data and optimizing W only gives s (cid:16) (cid:40)(cid:40)(cid:40)(cid:40)(cid:40)(cid:40)(cid:40)(cid:40) argmin Tr( W ⊤ A W ) + Tr( W ⊤ A W ) + 2Tr( W ⊤ A W ) s ss s s ss s s ss s W s (cid:24) (6) (cid:24)(cid:24)(cid:24)(cid:24) (cid:24) (cid:17) 2Tr( W ⊤ B ) 2Tr( W ⊤ B ) . − s s − s s The new objective in Equation 6 is quadratic, so we differentiate with respect to W and set the s derivative to zero to obtain the solution of the sub-matrix W = A − 1 ( B A W ) , (7) s ss s ss s − which gives us the sparse update rule in Algorithm 2. Concluding remark . So far, we have presented an efficient algorithm that learns world models online without forgetting. In our algorithm, linear models are exploited so that we can achieve no-regret online learning with Follow-The-Leader. To enlarge the model capacity, we devise a high-dimensional nonlinear random feature encoding, Losse, turning linear models into universal approximators (Huang et al., 2006). Moreover, the sparsity guarantee of Losse permits efficient updates of the shallow but wide model. We name our method Losse-FTL . We note that feature sparsity has been utilized to mitigate catastrophic forgetting for decades (McCloskey & Cohen, 1989; French, 1991; Liu et al., 2019; Lan & Mahmood, 2023). Our work differs from them in that not only does sparsity help reduce feature interference, but the incremental closed-form solution also guarantees the optimal fitting of all observed data, thus eliminating forgetting. More discussion on feature sparsity can be found in Appendix A. Another related line of research to ours is analytic class-incremental learning (Zhuang et al., 2022; 2023; 2024), which we discuss in Appendix B due to space constraints. The implementation details of Losse-FTL can be found in Appendix E.', 432, 1904, 'high')
ON CONFLICT DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO paper_text_chunks (chunk_id, paper_version_id, section_id, sequence_index, chunk_index_in_section, page_start, page_end, content_type, text_content, token_count, char_count, location_confidence)
VALUES ('paper_016_openreview_2024_02_11_chunk_0013', 'paper_016_openreview_2024_02_11', 'paper_016_openreview_2024_02_11_sec_4', 13, 0, 6, 6, 'body_text', 'In this section, we will showcase empirical results to validate the expressiveness of Losse (Section 4.1) and the online learning capability of Losse-FTL (Section 4.2), both in supervised learning settings.', 39, 206, 'high')
