INSERT INTO paper_text_chunks (chunk_id, paper_version_id, section_id, sequence_index, chunk_index_in_section, page_start, page_end, content_type, text_content, token_count, char_count, location_confidence)
VALUES ('paper_017_openreview_2024_02_11_chunk_0028', 'paper_017_openreview_2024_02_11', 'paper_017_openreview_2024_02_11_sec_d_1', 28, 0, 17, 18, 'body_text', 'Our formulation of the optimization objective is based on the framework established in (Ou et al., 2022). Specifically, the optimization objective is to address Equation 1 by adopting an implicit learning strategy grounded in probabilistic reasoning. This approach can be succinctly formulated as follows:

argmax E P [log p ( S ∗ | V )] ( V,S ) θ θ s . t . p ( S | V ) ∝ F ( S ; V ) , ∀ S ∈ 2 V , θ θ

The important step in addressing this problem involves constructing an appropriate set mass function p ( S | V ) that is monotonically increasing in relation to the utility function F ( S ; V ) . To achieve this, θ θ we can employ the Energy-Based Model (EBM): exp( F ( S ; V )) (cid:88) p ( S | V ) = θ , Z := exp( F ( S ′ ; V )) , θ Z θ S ′ ⊆ V In practice, we approximate the EBM by solving a variational approximation ϕ ∗ = argmin D ( q ( Y | S,V )) || p ( S | V )) , ϕ ϕ θ The expression of q ( Y | S,V ) is defined as follows: (cid:89) (cid:89) q ( Y | S,V ) = Y (1 − Y ) ,Y ∈ [0 , 1] | V | . i i i ∈ S i ̸∈ S Next, we would like to explain why q ( Y | S,V ) can approximate P ( S | V ) . We have defined that Y ∈ [0 , 1] | V | and S = { 0 , 1 } | V | . In this case, Y can be viewed as a stochastic version of S since Y can also be generated as Y = { 0 , 1 } | V | while still satisfying the constraint Y ∈ [0 , 1] | V | .

To facilitate comprehension, let us consider an illustrative scenario. Suppose we have a ground set V = { x ,x ,x } , and the optimal subset S ∗ is { x ,x } , which can be represented as [1 , 1 , 0] . 1 2 3 1 2 Specifically, we define P ( S ∗ | V ) = 1 , indicating that S ∗ is the correct subset, while for any S ̸ = S ∗ , we have P ( S | V ) = 0 . Now, let’s examine the case when Y = [1 , 1 , 0] . In this situation, we can calculate that q ( Y | S ∗ ,V ) = 1 . This implies that q ( Y | S ∗ ,V ) accurately represents the probability of observing S ∗ given V , and it correctly assigns a high probability to the optimal subset. Moreover, to generate Y, we construct an EquiNet (Ou et al., 2022), denoted as Y = EquiNet( V ; ϕ ) : 2 V → [0 , 1] | V | . This network takes the ground set V as input and outputs probabilities indicating the likelihood of each element x ∈ V being part of the optimal subset S ∗ . In the inference stage, EquiNet is employed to predict the optimal subset for a given ground set V , using a TopN rounding approach.

