Note that, we can also implement phantom edges for predicting p ( z ,..., z | X ) . This is done by i 1 i k noticing that belief propagation on factor graphs also produces marginals for factors, i.e., cliques of nodes. By adding a phantom node which is connected with i ,...,i , we have shown in the proof of 1 k Proposition 17, 18 that by applying MPNNs we can also learn the posteriori p ( z ,..., z | X ) , even i 1 i k if the nodes i ,...,i are not connected in the original graph. 1 k

Algorithm complexities. We now discuss the time and space complexities of the proposed phantom edges and compare with node labeling methods. Given a graph G with N nodes and M edges, we are asked to predict a batch of links E ˆ . Simultaneously predicting all links in E ˆ is equivalent to applying MPNNs on an induced graph with N nodes and M + | E| ˆ edges thus requires O ( N + M + | E| ˆ ) time. For labeling trick methods, they need to rerun GNNs for each target link thus requires O (ˆ E ( N + M )) time. For source node labeling methods, they need to rerun GNNs for target links with different source nodes. Therefore, suppose the target links E ˆ contains S different source nodes, they require O ( S ( N + M )) time.

Relation with node labeling methods. We now discuss the relation between our methods and node labeling methods. We will mainly focus on the ordered labeling trick (Zhang et al., 2020; Liu et al., 2021) and the partial node labeling (Zhu et al., 2021).

