We first show that labeling trick can be seen as a special case of the phantom edge. Suppose given a graph G , the target links E ˆ only contain one link E ˆ = { ( s,t ) } . In this case, we only add one phantom edge ( s,t ) into the original graph (Figure 1 (b)). Labeling trick methods add additional labels to mark s,t differently from the rest of the nodes (Figure 1 (a)). As we shall see later, both the two methods aim to mark the target link ( s,t ) uniquely: that is, for any graphs G,H and s,t ∈ V ,p,q ∈ V , obviously G H G ˆ ≃ H ˆ ⇐⇒ G ≃ H ⇐⇒ There exists an isomorphism π from G to H , π ( s ) = p,π ( t ) = q, (3) where G, ˆ H ˆ are graphs induced by phantom edges and G , H are graphs induced by labeling trick. Therefore, both the methods aim to tag the target link uniquely.', 852, 3102, 'high')
