INSERT INTO paper_text_chunks (chunk_id, paper_version_id, section_id, sequence_index, chunk_index_in_section, page_start, page_end, content_type, text_content, token_count, char_count, location_confidence)
VALUES ('paper_021_openreview_2024_02_11_chunk_0008', 'paper_021_openreview_2024_02_11', 'paper_021_openreview_2024_02_11_sec_3_4', 8, 0, 4, 4, 'body_text', 'We now define the meta-loss L and in particular, a novel variance-minimizing regularizer that meta substantially improves the ability of g ( · ) to capture model uncertainty. This meta-regularizer l ( θ,x ) eps is added to the cross-entropy classification loss l ( y,f ( x )) that is typically part of the meta-loss, θ leading to the following meta-objective on the specialized set ( X s , Y s ) : M (cid:88) L = L ( X ,Y ) + L ( θ,X ) = l ( y s ,f ( x s )) + l ( θ,x s ) (4) meta c s s eps s j θ j eps j j =1 Minimizing Bayesian Posterior uncertainty: We take inspiration from the Bayesian NN literature which regularizes the posterior on weight distribution so as to avoid overfitting or to embed extra domain knowledge. Unlike standard neural networks which output a point estimate for a given input, Bayesian networks (Buntine, 1991; Tishby et al., 1989; Denker et al., 1987; Blundell et al., 2015; Kwon et al., 2020) learn a distribution p ( ω | D ) over the neural network weights ω given the dataset D using maximum a posteriori probability (MAP) estimation. The predictive distribution for the output y ∗ , given the input x and D , can be then calculated by marginalisation as follows: p ( y ∗ | x ∗ ,D ) = (cid:82) p ( y ∗ | x ∗ ,ω ) p ( ω | D ) dω ≈ 1 (cid:80) K p ( y ∗ | x ∗ ,ω k ) . Here we utilize a recent K k =1 result (Gal & Ghahramani, 2016) that augmenting the training of a deterministic neural network with dropout regularization yields a variational approximation for a Bayesian Neural Network. At test time, taking multiple forward passes through the neural network for different dropout masks yields a Monte-Carlo approximation to Bayesian inference, and thereby a predictive distribution. The variance over these Monte Carlo samples is therefore a measure of predictive uncertainty: (cid:32) K (cid:33) 1 (cid:88) l ( θ,x ) ≈ ( f ( x ) − E [ f ( x )]) 2 (5) eps K D k ⊙ θ D k ⊙ θ k =1 where D denotes the dropout mask at k th sample and D ⊙ θ denotes the application of this dropout k k mask to the neural network parameters. This MCD measure is popular as an estimate of instance', 547, 2106, 'high')
