INSERT INTO paper_text_chunks (chunk_id, paper_version_id, section_id, sequence_index, chunk_index_in_section, page_start, page_end, content_type, text_content, token_count, char_count, location_confidence)
VALUES ('paper_022_openreview_2024_02_11_chunk_0008', 'paper_022_openreview_2024_02_11', 'paper_022_openreview_2024_02_11_sec_2_3', 8, 0, 4, 4, 'body_text', 'We use two publicly available datasets independently recorded by separate laboratories; we describe them here and provide more details in Appendix A.2. Networks were trained independently on each. F RIEDMAN 2017 Experimental data from Friedman et al. (2017) consists of the relative abundances of 2, 3, 7, and 8-bacteria communities (Fig. S3, Fig. S4, and Fig. S5). The dataset contains 93 samples with 2 to 15 replicates each. Raw data was kindly provided by Friedman et al. (2017) and is now available on our project webpage https://sites.google.com/view/microbegnn . B ARANWAL C LARK 2022 The dataset is published by Baranwal et al. (2022), with certain samples originally produced by Clark et al. (2021). The dataset is composed of relative abundances of 459 samples of 2 to 26-bacteria communities, each replicated 1 to 9 times. When testing generalization to excluded bacteria (see Sec. 3.3), we do not attempt to generalize to (i) Holdemanella biformis (HB) as the samples containing this bacterium are only present in two community sizes (2 and 26), resulting in a small test set, and (ii) Coprococcus comes (CC), Eubacterium rectale (ER), Roseburia intestinalis (RI), and Faecalibacterium praustnitzii (FP) due to their over-representation in samples, and so the resulting small training sets. Genomes of bacterial species were downloaded from NCBI (Sayers et al., 2022) or the ATCC Genome Portal (Yarmosh et al., 2022), annotated with the NCBI prokaryotic genome annotation pipeline Tatusova et al. (2016), and genes were mapped to the KEGG database to obtain functional groups (Moriya et al., 2007). When a specific strain’s genome was unavailable, the genome of the closest type strain was used instead. Details on strain genomes are provided in Supplementary Table S4. We used the presence/absence of each KO group as input for fitting models; KO annotations present in all genomes in a dataset were excluded.', 394, 1922, 'high')
ON CONFLICT DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO paper_text_chunks (chunk_id, paper_version_id, section_id, sequence_index, chunk_index_in_section, page_start, page_end, content_type, text_content, token_count, char_count, location_confidence)
VALUES ('paper_022_openreview_2024_02_11_chunk_0009', 'paper_022_openreview_2024_02_11', 'paper_022_openreview_2024_02_11_sec_2_4', 9, 0, 4, 5, 'body_text', 'We design a simulator for the growth of bacterial communities based on the generalized Lotka- Volterra model (see Sec. 2.1), to control data parameters and specifically assess the application of GNNs to bacterial communities. This simulator, as illustrated in Fig. 2, is not meant to produce a faithful representation of real communities, but rather to provide a generative procedure that captures certain challenges in the data, e.g. large dimensionality, while controlling other characteristics, e.g. sample size.

Sample community parameters Simulate Lotka-Volterra dynamics Simulated growth capacity interactions datasets Target: relative abundances Compute interaction vectors by minimizing at equilibrium

Concatenate and encode in binary Input: simulated genome passive active interactions interactions

Figure 2: Simulation of bacterial community growth dynamics and genomes. For each bacterium i in the community, we randomly draw its growth parameters: the growth rate µ , the carrying i capacity K , and its interaction factors a . From these parameters, we calculate the abundances of i i,. bacteria at equilibrium according to the generalized Lotka-Volterra equations. We use the relative abundances of bacteria at equilibrium as a learning target. In parallel, we simulate genomes by creating binary vectors that encode for the growth parameters. For that, we first approximate two vectors per bacterium of n dimensions: one to determine the effect of bacterium i on others, ν s , dim i and the other to determine the effect of other bacteria on i , ν r . For bacteria i and j , we assume that i the effect of i on j is expressed as a = ν s · ν r . The approximation is performed by minimizing the i,j i j distance between the real matrix of interactions and the one generated by the approximated vectors. We generate the binary encoding of the parameters µ , K , and the values in ν r and ν s . We also i i i i randomly draw a [0 − 1] vector to add noise to genomes. We use these “genomes” as input features to predict the relative abundances of bacteria at equilibrium.

Bacterial growth The growth of each bacterium in the community was simulated using the generalized Lotka-Volterra equation (Eq. 1), with: ln( µ ) ∼ N (1 , 0 . 5 2 ) clipped to [0 . 5 , 2] , K ∼ U (5 , 15) , i i and a ∼ Laplace(0 , 0 . 2 2 ) clipped to [ − 2 , 2] , ∀ i,j ∈ [1 , | S | ] . The target relative abundance i,j was calculated by simulating community growth until equilibrium: n (0) = 10 ∀ i ∈ [1 , | S | ] and i equilibrium was reached when d n / d t ≤ 10 − 3 ∀ i ∈ [1 , | S | ] (Fig. S1). Theoretically, this is similar i to solving the roots of Eq. 1 which implies that steady-states depend on the parameters K and i a ∀ i,j ∈ [1 , | S | ] . Given our set of parameters, all simulated communities were stable. i,j', 677, 2813, 'high')
ON CONFLICT DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO paper_text_chunks (chunk_id, paper_version_id, section_id, sequence_index, chunk_index_in_section, page_start, page_end, content_type, text_content, token_count, char_count, location_confidence)
VALUES ('paper_022_openreview_2024_02_11_chunk_0010', 'paper_022_openreview_2024_02_11', 'paper_022_openreview_2024_02_11_sec_2_4', 10, 1, 5, 5, 'body_text', 'Bacterial genomes Bacterial genomes are generated to encode the simulated growth parameters such that there exists an approximately bijective mapping from genomes to parameters. We achieve this by rescaling parameters to [0 , 1] , discretizing them, and performing a simple binary encoding to (cid:0) (cid:0) n (cid:1)(cid:1) n bits as g = bin round ( g − g ) / ( g − g ) · (2 g − 1) . Although the encoding is g bin min max min not representative of any biological process, the mapping can be computed efficiently, provides a compact representation, and can be inverted up to discretization. This method is applied directly to the parameters µ and K , resulting in two binary vectors of size n . g Encoding the interaction factors a into the genomes of each bacteria requires an additional step. i,j Given a bacterial community S , two intermediate n -dimensional vectors for each bacterium s ∈ S dim i are needed: one determining its effect on interaction partners, ν s ∈ R n , and the other determining i how it is affected by others, ν r ∈ R n . These vectors should contain sufficient information, such i that the influence of bacterium s on s (encoded in a ) can be retrieved from ν s and ν r . For i j i,j i j each pair of bacteria ( s ,s ) ∈ S 2 , we simply reconstruct interactions through inner products: i j a ˆ = ν s · ν r . We treat intermediate vectors as learnable parameters, and optimize them through i,j i j gradient descent by minimizing the distance of the reconstructed interaction matrix from its ground (cid:80) (cid:80) 2 truth: J = (ˆ a − a ) . The n vector coordinates for both vectors are finally i ∈ [1 , | S | ] j ∈ [1 , | S | ] i,j i,j dim encoded in the genomes as described above for µ and K . Here, we use n = 7 for all parameters, n = 20 for the 25 simulated bacteria, and add 5 % g dim of random genes. Empirically, we verify that µ , K , and ν s ,ν r can be accurately recovered from simulated genomes.', 500, 1938, 'high')
ON CONFLICT DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO paper_text_chunks (chunk_id, paper_version_id, section_id, sequence_index, chunk_index_in_section, page_start, page_end, content_type, text_content, token_count, char_count, location_confidence)
VALUES ('paper_022_openreview_2024_02_11_chunk_0011', 'paper_022_openreview_2024_02_11', 'paper_022_openreview_2024_02_11_sec_3', 11, 0, 5, 6, 'body_text', 'The general goal of this work is to train and evaluate neural models for the dynamics of bacterial communities, directly from their genomes. On real data (F RIEDMAN 2017 and B ARANWAL C LARK 2022),

A Generalize: in-distribution (CV) B Generalize: OOD community size 1 Model 1.0 Permuted Architecture Generalize to 0 FALSE MLP 0.5 bigger 2 R 2 R TRUE GraphSAGE 0.0 much bigger -1 MPGNN -0.5 smaller BaranwalClark2022 Friedman2017 BaranwalClark2022 Friedman2017 Figure 3: Accuracy of MLP and GNN models on predictions of in-distribution and bigger bacterial communities. Average R 2 values for predictions on in-distribution test sets with 95% confidence interval ( R 2 = 1 : perfect model, R 2 ≤ 0 : random model). The ensemble predictions across 5 model seeds were used and 5-folds CV was additionally used for A . B : Models were trained on communities of constrained sizes and tested on communities of distinct sizes.

we first investigate whether in-distribution predictions of unseen bacterial communities with known bacteria are possible. Then, we evaluate the generalization of learned models to (i) larger communities and (ii) unseen bacteria with respect to those used for training. Finally, due to the scarcity of real data, we leverage our proposed simulator to produce a dense and controllable distribution over communities: by retraining models on simulated data, we are able to validate whether trends emerging in real data can be explained in a simplified setting.', 308, 1479, 'high')
