Notice that the network N n +1 or N n +1 in expression 4 or 5 may be different for different n , which S D means we need to train a new neural network for each step. But if we impose the previously mentioned restrictions on the function g , we will discover the following wonderful fact. Since the boundary conditions of equation 3 are uniform for different n , the neural network N n +1 or N n +1 are the S D same for different n . This indicates that the rules to obtain u n +1 from u n are the same for every n : (cid:90) (cid:90) u n +1 ( x ) = v ( x ) + v ( x ) = G ( x,y ) u n ( y ) dy − G ( x,y ) N ( y,θ ) ds , (7) n +1 , 1 n +1 , 2 0 S y Ω ∂ Ω or (cid:90) (cid:90) ∂G ( x,y ) u n +1 ( x ) = v ( x ) + v ( x ) = G ( x,y ) u n ( y ) dy − 0 N ( y,θ ) ds . (8) n +1 , 1 n +1 , 2 ∂ n D y Ω ∂ Ω y This also means that if the Green’s function is known, we only need to train the neural network once to obtain all the u n +1 based on these uniform recursive formulas. If the Green’s function is unknown, we only need to train another neural network one more time based on the BI-GreenNet method by Lin et al. (2023a) to obtain the Green’s function of operator ( I − τ L ) and domain Ω . Remark.

