x (cid:88) ∞ ( x ) 2 n 1 1 K ( x ) = − (ln( ) + γ ) I ( x ) + 2 (1 + + ... + ) , (9) 0 2 0 ( n !) 2 2 n n =1 where γ is the Euler–Mascheroni constant with γ ≈ 0 . 5772156649 ... and I is the modified Bessel function of the first kind of order 0 which can be regarded as: ∞ ( x ) 2 n (cid:88) I ( x ) = 2 . (10) 0 ( n !) 2 n =0 For integral 4, the singularity happens when y nears x . For these y , we can approximate the value of G ( x,y ) according to some leading terms of K by equation 9. For d = 2 , the 0 0 variable y on ∂ Ω and near x can be parameterized by one parameter, and we just need to (cid:82) ϵ deal singular integral such as ln yh ( y ) dy where ϵ is a small positive number. This can − ϵ be handled as: (cid:90) ϵ (cid:90) ϵ ln yh ( y ) dy = h ( y ) d ( y ln y − y ) − ϵ − ϵ (cid:90) ϵ = h ( y )( y ln y − y ) | ϵ − h ′ ( y )( y ln y − y ) dy. − ϵ − ϵ And this form has no singularity since y ln( y ) − y → 0 as y → 0 . For d ≥ 3 , we can parameterize y ∈ ( x + ϵ B d − 1 ) (cid:84) ∂ Ω by polar coordinates (with ϵ being a small positive number), where B n is the n -dimensional unit ball.

Case 2: Semi-Linear Equations In this case, we want to solve PDE 1 with L ˜ = L + ϕ and ϕ ̸ = 0 , here L is an linear operator that the fundamental solution for I − k L ( k ∈ R ) can be obtained and + ϕ is a scalar function about u . We will use the idea of operator splitting to transform equation 1 to a linear PDE and an equation independent with the spatial differential, which can be regarded as an ordinary differential equation (ODE). In our method, we will use the Strang’s scheme of operator splitting for equation 1. In specifically, to solve PDE 1 in [ t n ,t n +1 ]( n ∈ Z , t n := nτ ) with a initial value u n and boundary ≥ 0 condition g , we can consider the three equations: ∂u ∗ = ϕ ( u ∗ ) for t ∈ [ t n ,t n + 1 2 ] , with u ∗ ( · ,t n ) = u n , (11) ∂t ∂u ∗∗ = L ( u ∗∗ ) for t ∈ [ t n ,t n +1 ] , with u ∗∗ ( · ,t n ) = u ∗ ( · ,t n + 1 2 ) , (12) ∂t ∂u ∗∗∗ = ϕ ( u ∗∗∗ ) for t ∈ [ t n + 1 2 ,t n +1 ] , with u ∗∗∗ ( · ,t n + 1 2 ) = u ∗∗ ( · ,t n +1 ) , (13) ∂t where t n + 1 2 := t n + 1 τ and u ∗ ,u ∗∗ ,u ∗∗∗ are functions defined on Ω × [ t n ,t n +1 ] . Note that above equations are needed to impose the appropriate boundary conditions given by function g . In this way, we can use function u ∗∗∗ ( · ,t n +1 ) to approximate the function u n +1 , which we desired as a numerical solution to PDE 1 at time t = ( n + 1) τ .

Note that the equations 11 and 13 can be integrated using standard ODE solvers either analytically or numerically. This together with the numerical solution of equation 12 using the approach in case 1 implies an alternating process for approximating the semi-linear PDE 1. The standard framework for NEKM is shown in Figure 1. Unique features of the NEKM', 1140, 3549, 'high')
