VALUES ('paper_023_openreview_2024_02_11_chunk_0015', 'paper_023_openreview_2024_02_11', 'paper_023_openreview_2024_02_11_sec_3_3', 15, 0, 8, 9, 'body_text', 'One noteworthy fact is that our method can be extended to PDEs on surfaces, as long as the operators on the surface and their corresponding fundamental solutions exist. An example of solving the heat equation on a hemisphere is provided below. Heat equation Suppose Ω = { ( ρ,θ,φ ) ∈ R × [0 , π ] × [0 , 2 π ] : ρ = 1 } is the episphere with + 2 the spherical coordinate: x = ρ sin( θ )cos( φ ) ,y = ρ sin( θ )sin( φ ) ,z = ρ cos( θ ) . In spherical coordinates, the Laplace-Beltrami operator instead of the common Laplacian is given by ∆ = s 1 1 ∂ 2 + 1 1 ∂ (sin θ ∂ ) . In this example, we consider heat equation on episphere with ρ 2 sin 2 θ ∂φ 2 ρ 2 sin θ ∂θ ∂θ initial condition u ( θ,φ,t = 0) = cos θ and zero boundary condition ( ∂ Ω = { ( ρ,θ,φ ) ∈ { 1 } × { π } × [0 , 2 π ] } ), whose exact solution is u ( θ,φ,t ) = e − 2 t cos θ . NEKM with the main framework

was used to solve it. We choose τ = 0 . 1 as the time step. Note that the detail expression of fundamental solution G of I − k ∆ ( k > 0) can be found in Appendix F and we can employ 0 s the method of images to compute the Green’s function G on the upper hemisphere with Dirichlet boundary conditions as follows: G (( θ,φ ) , ( θ ′ ,φ ′ )) = G (( θ,φ ) , ( θ ′ ,φ ′ )) − G (( θ,φ ) , ( π − θ ′ ,φ ′ )) . (15) 0 0 To get v (the result of ‘KF’ block) using BINet method, we use the fully connected neural netn +1 , 2 work with 6 hidden layers and 100 neurons per layer with tanh activation functions. For computing the loss function, we choose equidistant 400 sample points on ∂ Ω . To get v (the result of ‘GF’ n +1 , 1 block), we can use the known Green’s function G and integral 6. Some results are shown in figure 6. To compute the error, we uniformly set up 80 ∗ 320 sampling points in the domain Ω at time t = 0 . 1 , 0 . 2 ,..., 0 . 5 and calculate the function values of the predict solution and the exact solution. By computing, the absolute L 2 error over ( θ,ϕ,t ) ∈ [0 , π ] × [0 , 2 π ] × [0 , 0 . 5] is 0 . 0188670 .

Figure 6: Solution for the heat equation at time t = 0 , 0 . 1 , 0 . 2 . These figures compare the solutions given by NEKM and the exact solutions.

Allen-Cahn equation In this part, we employ NEKM to solve the Allen-Cahn equation u ( θ,φ,t ) = ∆ u ( θ,φ,t )+ 1 ( u − u 3 )( ϵ = 0 . 1) defined on episphere with zero Neumann boundary t s ϵ 2 condition and random initial value between − 0 . 1 and 0 . 1 , which simulates the processes of phase separation. We present the results of solving this equation using NEKM directly in figure 7, as the process of solving it is similar with solving the heat equation in the previous section except the ODE solver processes which are detailed in Appendix D.1. It can be observed that the solution of this equation actually simulates phase separation process.

Figure 7: Predict solution for the Allen-Cahn equation. These figures show the predict solution of Allen-Cahn equation given by NEKM at time t = 0 . 1 , 0 . 2 , 0 . 3 and 0 . 4 .', 878, 2981, 'high')
