INSERT INTO paper_text_chunks (chunk_id, paper_version_id, section_id, sequence_index, chunk_index_in_section, page_start, page_end, content_type, text_content, token_count, char_count, location_confidence)
VALUES ('paper_023_openreview_2024_02_11_chunk_0021', 'paper_023_openreview_2024_02_11', 'paper_023_openreview_2024_02_11_sec_a_2', 21, 0, 13, 13, 'body_text', 'The Green’s function is a crucial tool in the analysis and solution of PDEs. However, obtaining an analytical solution of the Green’s function for a general operator and domain is nearly impossible due to its dependence on both the operator and the shape of the domain. Furthermore, the variables involved in the Green’s function are often high-dimensional, making it difficult for traditional numerical methods to provide effective solutions. Despite these challenges, the development of methods for obtaining the Green’s function for various operators and domains has remained an active research area, as it can provide valuable insights into the behavior of solutions to PDEs and facilitate the development of numerical methods for solving these equations. In this subsection, we propose a method, boundary integral-Green network by Lin et al. (2023a) for computing the Green’s function based on the BINet approach. Let Ω ⊂ R d be a bounded domain, we consider the PDE: (cid:26) L u ( x ) = f ( x ) in Ω , (22) u ( x ) = g ( x ) on ∂ Ω , where L is a differential operator. To find the Green’s function G ( x,y ) of this equation, we firstly define H ( x,y ) = G ( x,y ) − G ( x,y ) , where G is the fundamental solution of the operator L (in 0 0 space R d ). Note that the definition of Green’s function and its difference from the fundamental solution can be found in Appendix B of this paper. Consider that G ( x,y ) vanishes when x goes to ∂ Ω , we only care about the value of H ( x,y ) for ( x,y ) ∈ Ω \ ∂ Ω × Ω . Then we can get the function for H as following: (cid:26) L H ( x,y ) = 0 , ∀ x ∈ Ω \ ∂ Ω ,y ∈ Ω , y (23) H ( x,y ) = − G ( x,y ) , ∀ x ∈ Ω \ ∂ Ω ,y ∈ ∂ Ω . One observation is that for each x ∈ Ω \ ∂ Ω , H ( x, · ) is a function with one variable in Ω ⊂ R d , then the BINet method can be using to find it. Therefore we can use the form of single or double potential to approximate the solution H . That is, (cid:90) H ( x,y ) ≈ S [ h ]( x,y ) := − G ( y,z ) h ( x,z ) ds , (24) 0 z ∂ Ω or (cid:90) ∂G ( y,z ) H ( x,y ) ≈ D [ h ]( x,y ) := − 0 h ( x,z ) ds (25) ∂ n z ∂ Ω z can be used to approximate the solution of equation 23. In this case, if we use a neural network N ( x,y ; θ ) to estimate function h ( x,y )( x ∈ Ω \ ∂ Ω ,y ∈ ∂ Ω) , the loss function can be set as  N  (cid:80) |S [ N (: ,θ )]( x ,y ) + G ( x ,y ) | 2 , single layer potential i i 0 i i L ( θ ) = i =1 (26) N  (cid:80) |D [ N (: ,θ )]( x ,y ) + 1 N ( x ,y ; θ ) + G ( x ,y ) | 2 , double layer potential i i 2 i i 0 i i i =1 where { x ,y } N are N points randomly sampled in Ω \ ∂ Ω × ∂ Ω . After finding the most ‘approi i j =1 priate’ function h through training, we can get the solution H and therefore the Green’s function G by G = H + G . We are now able to find the Green’s function of an operator in a general domain as long as the fundamental solution of the operator is known. This is not only an excellent achievement, but also provides some algorithmic support for our later work on solving time-evolving PDEs.', 910, 3026, 'high')
ON CONFLICT DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO paper_text_chunks (chunk_id, paper_version_id, section_id, sequence_index, chunk_index_in_section, page_start, page_end, content_type, text_content, token_count, char_count, location_confidence)
VALUES ('paper_023_openreview_2024_02_11_chunk_0022', 'paper_023_openreview_2024_02_11', 'paper_023_openreview_2024_02_11_sec_b', 22, 0, 13, 14, 'body_text', 'In this part, we make some supplementary introductions to the fundamental solution and Green’s function. Let Ω ⊂ R d be a bounded domain, Ω C := R d \ Ω ¯ and Ω ∗ := Ω or Ω C . L is a differential operator. (cid:26) 0 , x ̸ = ⃗ 0 , Definition 1. A function δ ( x ) is called a d -dimensional δ -function if δ ( x ) ≃ and ∞ , x = ⃗ 0 , (cid:82) which is also constrained to satisfy the identity δ ( x ) dx = 1 . For all function f that is continuous R d at a ∈ R d we have (cid:82) f ( x ) δ ( x − a ) dx = f ( a ) . R d

Definition 2. A function G ( x,y ) is called the fundamental solution corresponding to equations L u ( x ) = 0 if G ( x,y ) is symmetric about x and y and G ( x,y ) satisfy L G ( x,y ) = δ ( x − y ) , 0 0 y 0 where ( x,y ) ∈ R d × R d and L is the differential operator L which acts on component y. y Definition 3. A function G ( x,y ) is called the Green’s function corresponding to problem

(cid:26) L u ( x ) = f ( x ) in Ω ∗ , u ( x ) = g ( x ) on ∂ Ω ∗ ,

if G ( x,y ) is a 2 d -dimensional function satisfying

(cid:26) L G ( x,y ) = δ ( x − y ) , ∀ x,y ∈ Ω ∗ , y . G ( x,y ) = 0 , ∀ x ∈ Ω ∗ ,y ∈ ∂ Ω ∗ .

In addition, if the type of boundary condition in the problem is changed, the boundary conditions that Green’s function needed to satisfy must also be changed to the corresponding zero boundary condition.

By the definition of fundamental solution and Green’s function, the differences between them is clear and easy to understand. Fundamental solution is only depend on the operator while Green’s function is depend both on the operator and the boundary condition.', 502, 1598, 'high')
