Implementation Settings. We compare BATTLE with existing state-of-the-art adversarial attack methods. To achieve fair comparison, we make simple adjustments for SA-RL and PA-AD to suit our settings in the experiments. In their original version, both of these two methods use the negative value of the reward obtained by the victim to train an adversary. We replace it with the same estimated reward function r as our method uses, which means they also learn from human preferences. (cid:98) ψ Following the settings in PEBBLE (Lee et al., 2021a), we use a scripted teacher that provides ground truth preference labels. More details of scripted teacher and preference collection can be found in Appendix E. For the implementation of SA-RL 1 and PA-AD 2 , we use the released official codebase. For fair comparison, all methods learned via PbRL are given the same number of preference labels. In the manipulation scenario, we use 9000 labels for all tasks. In the opposite behaviors scenario, we use 1000 for Window Close, 3000 for Drawer Close, 5000 for Faucet Open, Faucet Close and Window Open, 7000 for Drawer Open, Door Lock and Door Unlock. Also, to reduce the impact of PbRL, we additionally add oracle versions of SA-RL and PA-AD, which uses the ground-truth rewards of the targeted task.', 941, 3350, 'high')
