Figure 3: Illustration of LongHorizonUI framework. The LongGUIBench is introduced to define complex long-horizon interaction scenarios. An enhanced perceiver (OCR + Icon detection) extracts enriched UI element features, while a deep reasoning engine performs three-tier closed-loop validation of action feasibility. The compensation actuator employs multi-stage strategies (index/relative/absolute/historical coordinates) for robust execution.

screen images to simulate real player decision flows. Each task includes two levels of instructions: High-Level instructions (HL) define macro goals, such as “purchase item XX in the game store," while Low-Level instructions (LL) are broken down into atomic operation sequences, such as “click the store button," and “click the purchase button." Additionally, every operation step is annotated with fine-grained UI metadata, including control type (e.g., button, text box, drop-down menu), bbox coordinates, and state attributes.', 598, 3344, 'high')
ON CONFLICT DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO paper_text_chunks (chunk_id, paper_version_id, section_id, sequence_index, chunk_index_in_section, page_start, page_end, content_type, text_content, token_count, char_count, location_confidence)
VALUES ('paper_031_iclr_2026_proceedings_chunk_0006', 'paper_031_iclr_2026_proceedings', 'paper_031_iclr_2026_proceedings_sec_2_1', 6, 1, 4, 4, 'body_text', 'General Scenarios. To assess generalization capability, we constructed 147 end-to-end task chains across 15 popular apps, covering complete user workflows from trigger to feedback. Each task requires 15-27 actions (mean = 19.5) and incorporates both abstraction levels: High-level instructions define global goals (e.g., ’Schedule a 1.5-hour meeting starting at 10 am on June 29th’). Lowlevel instructions specify atomic operations (e.g., Launch Tencent Meeting; Click ’Schedule’; Select ’Standard Meeting’; Set duration). All steps are annotated with granular UI semantics, emphasizing complex interface behaviours (e.g., multi-level dropdown navigation, real-time input validation) to validate long-horizon GUI agents in challenging workflows.', 126, 745, 'high')
ON CONFLICT DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO paper_text_chunks (chunk_id, paper_version_id, section_id, sequence_index, chunk_index_in_section, page_start, page_end, content_type, text_content, token_count, char_count, location_confidence)
VALUES ('paper_031_iclr_2026_proceedings_chunk_0007', 'paper_031_iclr_2026_proceedings', 'paper_031_iclr_2026_proceedings_sec_2_2', 7, 0, 4, 5, 'body_text', 'Accurately identifying and disambiguating interactive elements in context is key to enabling task automation in complex GUIs. To this end, we propose the Multimodal Enhanced Perceiver (MEP), which unifies icon detection, OCR recognition, and heuristic repair into an ID-centred abstraction layer, extracting actionable signals from evolving GUIs, inspired by prior work (Lu et al., 2024c). Specifically, given a GUI screenshot S , MEP extracts visual elements through parallel perception modules: (i) An enhanced detector identifies interactive controls, producing E = ( id ,b ,c ) N , ui i i i i =1 with id a unique spatial tag, b its bounding box, and c the confidence from the detector head i i i (sigmoid class probability). IDs serve as stable anchors, robust to small layout variations. MEP also highlightspreviouslyclickedelements.(ii)AconventionalOCRmoduleextracts E = ( t ,b ) M , text j j j =1 with t the detected text and b the bounding box. To disambiguate composite controls such as icon j j + text, each e ∈ E is linked with its most relevant text via a semantic binding function: i ui { ( id , b ∪ b ∗ , t ∗ , c ) , if IoU( b ,b ∗ ) ≥ τ, i i j j i i j e ˆ = Φ( e ,E ) = (1) i i text ∅ ( id , b , , c ) , otherwise , i i i

where j ∗ = argmax IoU( b ,b ) denotes the text box with maximum overlap, and binding is apj i j plied only when IoU( b ,b ∗ ) ≥ τ (Appendix D.3). i j

To mitigate missed detections of critical elements, such as close buttons on pop-ups, we employ a fallback template matcher that is activated when no elements are detected in designated high-priority areas A (small normalized bands around pop-up corners and bottom bars where missing a priority control can stall a trajectory). Upon activation, the module invokes a repair function R over A , priority leveraging a template library T of canonical close/cancel, confirm/next, and back/home icons; highsimilarity matches are inserted as new elements only in these regions to match and restore omitted key elements.', 478, 2002, 'high')
ON CONFLICT DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO paper_text_chunks (chunk_id, paper_version_id, section_id, sequence_index, chunk_index_in_section, page_start, page_end, content_type, text_content, token_count, char_count, location_confidence)
VALUES ('paper_031_iclr_2026_proceedings_chunk_0008', 'paper_031_iclr_2026_proceedings', 'paper_031_iclr_2026_proceedings_sec_2_3', 8, 0, 5, 5, 'body_text', 'Current agent decision mechanisms (Niu et al., 2024; Kil et al., 2024) based on self-supervised training paradigms exhibit limited long-horizon generalization due to constrained dataset diversity, while MLLMs-based mechanisms (Wang et al., 2024b), despite superior sequence modeling capabilities, suffer from cascading error propagation under dynamic interface shifts, compromising reliability in long-horizon task execution. To address this, we propose the Deep Reflection Decider, as illustrated in Figure 4, which implements a structured multi-level feedback mechanism to establish triple closed-loop reasoning. This strategy validates goal rationality pre-execution and confirms environmental-state consistency post-execution, ensuring action precision and prediction credibility. Specifically, a strictly defined JSON Schema (fields: historical_status , Acition prompt ： Tap the ''Use'' button for the preset by CIKE14 import_contents , think , Execute_goal , Evaluation Base this ONLY on the screen image, evaluate if the action , further details are provided in Ap- prev_goal previous action visually achieved its intended goal. pendix E.1.) enforces structured three-tier reason- Import Output important contents closely related to user\''s ing, where the first three fields implement reflection contents instruction on the current page. and the last two fields implement decision: Provide a step-by-step thinking process. Analyze the current Think screen, relate it to the overall task and the visual outcome of the previous step . Decide the next best single action… (1) Historical Validation : the historical status validates UI state transitions (e.g., button Next_goal riefly describe the specific, immediate goal of the next action you are proposing in the ''action'' field. activation, text input) via OCR/icon detection, establishing spatiotemporal verification loops. Action "action_name": { dictionary of parameters for the action } Failure flags trigger root-cause analysis upon detecting error dialogues or unresponsive elements. (2) Target Check : the import_contents field extracts screen-critical information, validating the Figure 4: Deep reflection and decision- MLLM’senvironmentalcomprehensionviaOCR/i- making processes designed to validate prior con detection. Task-goal consistency assessments actions and predict subsequent steps. retain high-relevance text while filtering noise. (3) Action Explainability : the think field requires the MLLM to sequentially analyze current UI states, failure causes (if any), and action localization rationale (e.g., “Button #12 has the highest interaction confidence”), with outputs culminating in executable goals ( Execute_goal ) that are translated into atomic actions ( action ).

Pre-execution Reflection. Before execution, each candidate action is screened for on-screen grounding and task entailment. We accept a for execution only if [ ] [ ] ϕ ( s ,a | G , T ) = 1 g ( a ) ∈ G ∧ 1 K ( d ) ⊆ K ( T ) = 1 . (2) t t tg t action

Here, G denotes the UI elements at state s from the perceiver, T the global task description, and t t a a candidate action with ( Execute_goal , action ) and description d . g ( a ) is the target action tg elementof a , and K ( · ) akeywordextractorenforcesthattheactionsemanticsareconsistentwiththe task. In practice, if either the target element is absent from the current screen or the action semantics are not entailed by the task description, the action is rejected and a brief revision step is triggered using available OCR/icon evidence; otherwise, the action proceeds.', 688, 3571, 'high')
