VALUES ('paper_033_iclr_2026_proceedings_chunk_0008', 'paper_033_iclr_2026_proceedings', 'paper_033_iclr_2026_proceedings_sec_3', 8, 0, 3, 4, 'body_text', 'As shown in Eqs. (1) and (2), (dual) smooth CE is related to improvements in the population risk, and Błasiok et al. (2023) utilized these relationships to analyze when algorithms can achieve a small

smooth CE. However, their results are defined over population-level quantities, where the expectation by D is taken, making them inapplicable to practical algorithms trained on finite data points.

Since our goal is to analyze the behavior of the smooth CE under standard learning algorithms trained on finite data, it is natural to consider its empirical counterpart. Given a dataset S = { ( X ,Y ) } n n i i i =1 consisting of independently and identically distributed (i.i.d.) data points sampled from the data distribution D , we define the empirical smooth CE (Błasiok et al., 2023) given S as n n 1 (cid:88) smCE( f,S ) := sup h ( f ( X )) · ( Y − f ( X )) . n n i i i h ∈ Lip ([0 , 1] , [ − 1 , 1]) i =1 We also express this as smCE( f,S ) = sup ⟨ h ( f ( X )) ,Y − f ( X ) ⟩ . We n h ∈ Lip ([0 , 1] , [ − 1 , 1]) L ( S ) 1 2 n similarly define smCE σ ( g,S ) as empirical counterparts of smCE σ ( g, D ) . n We consider two datasets: a training set S = { Z } n ∼ D n , used to learn the predictor f , and tr i i =1 an independent test set S = { Z ′ } n ∼ D n . We call smCE( f,S ) as the training smooth CE , te i i =1 tr smCE( f,S ) as the test smooth CE , and smCE( f, D ) as the population smooth CE . Given S , te tr we are interested in evaluating | smCE( f, D ) − smCE( f,S ) | . Błasiok et al. (2023) has shown that tr √ | smCE( f, D ) − smCE( f,S ) | = O (1 / n ) . (3) te p Therefore, we need to evaluate | smCE( f,S ) − smCE( f,S ) | , which we refer to as the smooth te tr CE generalization gap . Combining this gap with Eq. (3), we obtain the desired bound. To evaluate the smooth CE generalization gap, we use the covering number bound (Wainwright, 2019). Suppose that F is equipped with the metric ∥·∥ . Let N ( ϵ, F , ∥·∥ ) be an ϵ -cover with metric ∥·∥ of F , ∞ ∞ ∞ with the cardinality N ( ϵ, F , ∥ · ∥ ) . Then, for any f ∈ F , there exists f ˜ ∈ N ( ϵ, F , ∥ · ∥ ) such ∞ ∞ that ∥ f − f ˜ ∥ ≤ ϵ . ∞ We now present our first main result: (All proofs for this section are provided in Appendix C.) Theorem 1. Let S ∼ D n and S ∼ D n be independent test and training datasets. Then, for any te tr δ > 0 , with probability at least 1 − δ over the draw of S and S , we have: te tr (cid:90) (cid:114) (cid:114) 1 ln N ( ϵ ′ , F , ∥ · ∥ ) log δ − 1 sup | smCE( f,S ) − smCE( f,S ) |≤ inf 8 ϵ +24 ∞ dϵ ′ +2 . te tr n n f ∈F ϵ ≥ 0 ϵ ′

