The first term on the right-hand side reflects the complexity of Lip ([0 , 1] , [ − 1 , 1]) , while the second corresponds to F . A direct application of uniform convergence theory would yield a bound in terms of R (Lip ([0 , 1] , [ − 1 , 1]) ◦F ) , which cannot be reduced to R ( F ) using the standard contraction D ,n 1 D ,n

lemma. Our analysis circumvents this difficulty by employing a covering argument for Lipschitz functions. Similarly, Rademacher complexity can be used to bound | smCE( f,S ) − smCE( f,S ) | . te tr However, such a bound depends on the complexity of the Lipschitz function, making it suboptimal relative to Theorem 1. See Appendix D for a detailed comparison between Corollary 1 and Theorem 2.

In conclusion, by jointly controlling the complexity of the hypothesis class and reducing the training smooth CE, we guarantee a small population smooth CE. In the next section, we show how to achieve a small training smooth CE via functional gradients.', 443, 1666, 'high')
