INSERT INTO paper_text_chunks (chunk_id, paper_version_id, section_id, sequence_index, chunk_index_in_section, page_start, page_end, content_type, text_content, token_count, char_count, location_confidence)
VALUES ('paper_033_iclr_2026_proceedings_chunk_0010', 'paper_033_iclr_2026_proceedings', 'paper_033_iclr_2026_proceedings_sec_4', 10, 0, 5, 5, 'body_text', 'In this section, we discuss how we can minimize the training smooth CE. From Eqs. (1) and (2), (dual) smooth CE can be characterized via the (dual) post-processing gap, which describes the potential improvement over functions . Building on this perspective, we show that both smooth and dual smooth CEs can be further characterized using functional gradients . Since we focus on the functional gradients over the training dataset, they are just finite-dimensional vectors. We provide the precise connection between functional gradients and the population smooth CE in Appendix I. For the squared loss, the gradient for the predicted probability is ∇ ℓ ( f,y ) = f − y , and for the f sq cross-entropy loss, the gradient for the logit is ∇ ℓ ( σ ( g ) ,y ) = σ ( g ) − y . Using these, we can g ent write the smooth and dual smooth CEs on the training dataset S as tr smCE( f,S ) = sup ⟨ h ( f ( X )) , −∇ ℓ ( f ( X ) ,Y ) ⟩ , tr f sq L 2 ( S n ) h ∈ Lip ([0 , 1] , [ − 1 , 1]) smCE σ ( g,S ) = sup ⟨ h ( g ( X )) , −∇ ℓ ( σ ( g ( X )) ,Y ) ⟩ . tr g ent L 2 ( S n ) h ∈ Lip ( R , [ − 1 , 1]) 1 / 4

The connection between functional gradients and the smooth CE can be extended to general proper scoring rules using the post-processing gaps, see Appendix I for the details.

Therefore, to effectively control the training smooth CE, it is natural to consider algorithms that focus on functional gradients. From this perspective, in the following subsections, we investigate how the smooth CE interacts with the training dynamics of three widely used models: gradient boosting tree, kernel boosting in a reproducing kernel Hilbert space (RKHS), and two-layer neural networks. These models can all be interpreted on the basis of functional gradients, and our theoretical framework enables a unified understanding of their calibration behavior under different forms of regularization. In this section, we express the empirical risk as L ( g ) = 1 (cid:80) n ℓ ( σ ( g ( X )) ,Y ) n n i =1 ent i i and the functional gradient over the training dataset as ∇ L ( g ) = g n ( ∇ ℓ ( σ ( g ( X )) ,Y )) ,..., ∇ ℓ ( σ ( g ( X )) ,Y )) ∈ R n . g ent 1 1 g ent n n', 595, 2150, 'high')
