INSERT INTO paper_text_chunks (chunk_id, paper_version_id, section_id, sequence_index, chunk_index_in_section, page_start, page_end, content_type, text_content, token_count, char_count, location_confidence)
VALUES ('paper_033_iclr_2026_proceedings_chunk_0014', 'paper_033_iclr_2026_proceedings', 'paper_033_iclr_2026_proceedings_sec_4_3', 14, 0, 8, 8, 'body_text', 'The next example is the two-layer neural network (NN). While NNs are typically trained by gradient descent on their parameters, recent work (Nitanda et al., 2019) shows that under certain hyperparameter settings—such as output scaling and a sufficiently large number of neurons—two-layer NNs behave similarly to kernel boosting with the neural tangent kernel (NTK). We adopt the setting of Nitanda et al. (2019). Define the logit function g : X → R by a two-layer θ NN g ( x ) = 1 (cid:80) m a ϕ ( θ · x ) , where m > 0 is the number of hidden units, β ∈ [0 , 1] is a θ m β r =1 r r scaling exponent, and ϕ : R → R is a smooth activation function (e.g., sigmoid, tanh). The weights { a } m ∈ {− 1 , 1 } m are fixed, while the parameters θ = { θ } m with θ ∈ R d are updated via r r =1 r r =1 r gradient descent: θ ( t +1) = θ ( t ) − w ∇ L ( g ) with constant stepsize w > 0 . θ n θ ( t ) We now give an informal statement of Theorem 2 from Nitanda et al. (2019), which provides guarantees for the functional gradient. Assume: (1) the activation function is smooth; (2) the initial parameters are sampled from a sub-Gaussian distribution; (3) the NTK-transformed data are separable with margin γ ; (4) the stepsize is sufficiently small; and (5) the number of hidden units m is large enough. Then, for T ≤ mγ 2 K 3 , there exist constants K , K , and K such that, for all β ∈ [0 , 1] , w 1 2 3 with probability at least 1 − δ over the random initialization, the following bound holds: T − 1 (cid:18) 2 β − 1 (cid:19) 1 (cid:88) K m ∥∇ ℓ ( g ( X ) ,Y ) ∥ 2 ≤ 1 + K . (6) T g ent θ ( t ) L 1 ( S n ) γ 2 T w 2 t =0 See Appendix H for the formal statement. Then, similar to Theorem 4, we have (cid:115) T − 1 (cid:18) 2 β − 1 (cid:19) 1 (cid:88) K m min smCE σ ( g ,S ) ≤ smCE σ ( g ,S ) ≤ 1 + K . θ ( t ) tr T θ ( t ) tr γ 2 T w 2 t ∈{ 0 ,...,T − 1 } t =0 Combining the Rademacher complexity estimate of Nitanda et al. (2019) with Theorem 2, we derive upper bounds on the population smooth CE and misclassification rate, analogous to the kernel boosting setting (see Appendix H for details). As in Theorem 4, the bound reveals a trade-off in T : increasing T reduces the training smooth CE but enlarges the complexity term, which grows at rate √ O ( wT ) . Similar to Corollary 4, we further show that with suitable hyperparameters, both ϵ -smooth CE and ϵ -classification error can be guaranteed. Corollary 5 (Informal) . Suppose the same assumptions as those for Eq. (6) hold. If for any ϵ > 0 , the hyperparameters satisfy one of the following: (i) β ∈ [0 , 1) , m = Ω( γ 1 − − 2 β ϵ 1 − − 1 β ) , T = Ω( γ − 2 ϵ − 2 ) , w = Θ( γ − 2 ϵ − 2 T − 1 m 2 β − 1 ) , n = Ω( ˜ γ − 2 ϵ − 4 ) , (ii) β = 0 , m = Θ (cid:0) γ − 2 ϵ − 3 / 2 log(1 /ϵ ) (cid:1) , T = Θ (cid:0) γ − 2 ϵ − 1 log 2 (1 /ϵ ) (cid:1) , w = Θ( m − 1 ) , n = Ω( ˜ ϵ − 2 ) , then with probability at least 1 − δ , gradient descent with the stepsize w finds a parameter θ ( t ) satisfying smCE( σ ( g ) ,S ) ≤ ϵ and P [(2 Y − 1) g ( X ) ≤ 0] ≤ ϵ within T iterations. θ ( t ) tr ( X,Y ) ∼D θ ( t )

