INSERT INTO paper_text_chunks (chunk_id, paper_version_id, section_id, sequence_index, chunk_index_in_section, page_start, page_end, content_type, text_content, token_count, char_count, location_confidence)
VALUES ('paper_033_iclr_2026_proceedings_chunk_0026', 'paper_033_iclr_2026_proceedings', 'paper_033_iclr_2026_proceedings_sec_b', 26, 0, 15, 15, 'body_text', 'Here, we introduce several definitions of calibration metrics and their relationship to the smooth CE, which is the primary focus of our paper.', 30, 143, 'high')
ON CONFLICT DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO paper_text_chunks (chunk_id, paper_version_id, section_id, sequence_index, chunk_index_in_section, page_start, page_end, content_type, text_content, token_count, char_count, location_confidence)
VALUES ('paper_033_iclr_2026_proceedings_chunk_0027', 'paper_033_iclr_2026_proceedings', 'paper_033_iclr_2026_proceedings_sec_b_1', 27, 0, 15, 16, 'body_text', 'Given a distribution D and predictor f , we consider the distribution induced by f , which is the joint distribution of prediction-label pairs ( f ( x ) ,y ) ∈ [0 , 1] × { 0 , 1 } , denoted by D . f Definition 5 (Perfect calibration (Błasiok et al., 2023)) . We say that a prediction-label distribution Γ over [0 , 1] × { 0 , 1 } is perfectly calibrated if E [ y | v ] = v . Moreover, given D over X × { 0 , 1 } , ( v,y ) ∼ Γ we say that f is perfectly calibrated with respect to D if D is perfectly calibrated. f

We denote the set of perfectly calibrated predictors with respect to D by cal( D ) .

Next, we introduce the true calibration distance, defined as the distance to the closest calibrated predictor: Definition 6 (True calibration distance) . The true distance of a predictor f from calibration is defined as

dCE ( f ) := inf E | f ( x ) − g ( x ) | . D D g ∈ cal( D )

This is the l metric, which possesses many desirable properties for measuring calibration; see Błasiok et al. (2023) for details. However, it is not practically usable because cal( D ) may be non-convex, and the metric depends on the domain X , whereas calibration metrics typically depend only on µ ( D ,f ) .

Błasiok et al. (2023) proposed that any calibration metric should satisfy the following three criteria:

(i) Prediction-only access A calibration metric should depend only on the distribution over ( f ( x ) ,y ) ∼ D , and not on the distribution over X . If it meets this requirement, we say that a f calibration metric µ satisfies the Prediction-only access (PA) property. Note that the true calibration distance depends on ( x,f ( x ) ,y ) and is therefore called the sample access (SA) model.

(ii) Consistency Definition 7. For c > 0 , a calibration metric µ is said to satisfy c -robust completeness if there exists a constant a ≥ 0 such that for every distribution D on X × { 0 , 1 } , and predictor f ∈ F µ ( D ,f ) ≤ a (dCE ( f )) c . D Definition 8. For s > 0 , a calibration metric µ satisfies s -robust soundness if there exists a constant b ≥ 0 such that for every distribution D on X × { 0 , 1 } , and predictor f ∈ F , µ ( D ,f ) ≥ b (dCE ( f )) s . D Definition 9. A calibration metric µ is said to be ( c,s ) -consistent if it satisfies both c -robust completeness and s -robust soundness.

Thus, consistent calibration metrics are polynomially related to the true distance from calibration. Błasiok et al. (2023) showed that any PA model must satisfy s/c ≥ 2 .

(iii) Efficiency A calibration metric µ is said to be efficient if it can be computed to accuracy ϵ in time poly (1 /ϵ ) using poly (1 /ϵ ) random samples from D . f Błasiok et al. (2023) showed that the smooth CE satisfies all of these properties. In particular, with respect to consistency, the smooth CE satisfies (cid:112) smCE( f, D ) ≤ dCE ( f ) ≤ 4 2smCE( f, D ) D

