VALUES ('paper_034_iclr_2026_proceedings_chunk_0018', 'paper_034_iclr_2026_proceedings', 'paper_034_iclr_2026_proceedings_sec_3_2', 18, 0, 9, 10, 'body_text', 'C LASSIFICATION

We evaluate the predictive performance of TimeSliver Table 4: Accuracy (acc.) of vs. 16 TimeSliver ( f cls ) on 26 datasets from baselines on 26 UEA datasets. Bold : best, Underlined: the UEA multivariate time-series classifica- second-best. tion archive (Ruiz et al., 2021), which span Type Method Bio. Motion Audio Coord. Misc. All 8 electrical biosignal (Bio.) datasets, 3 audio datasets, 7 accelerometer-based mo- DTW_D 46.9 87.5 44.5 95.4 63.9 66.8 Distance- DTW_I 47.9 77.1 34.4 90.7 59.2 61.3 tion datasets, 3 gesture and digit recogni- Based DTW_A 45.4 88.1 63.3 95.4 71.3 69.7 tion datasets in Cartesian coordinates (Coord.), Dictionary- MUSE 55.7 88.3 51.8 96.0 81.7 72.3 and other miscellaneous datasets and evalu- gRSF 49.0 84.3 46.1 88.3 71.7 63.8 Based ate TimeSliver against five methodologi- CIF 54.0 85.4 55.1 96.2 83.0 71.5 cal categories: Distance-based methods (Bag- Feature- MrSEQL 52.3 87.8 47.6 94.2 76.6 69.2 nall et al., 2016); Dictionary/interval-based BasedML ROCKET 53.8 90.1 48.7 96.6 75.6 70.2 methods (Schäfer & Leser, 2017); Feature- TapNet 49.9 84.5 51.5 91.5 65.4 64.8 based ML models; Deep learning models in- ResNet 48.3 90.6 47.5 97.3 57.2 63.3 IncTime 60.3 96.4 61.6 95.0 69.1 74.3 cluding ResNet (Wang et al., 2017), Inception- FCN 56.76 90.8 58.4 97.8 63.4 72.2 Time (Fawaz et al., 2020), FCN (Karim et al., TS2Vec 48.57 87.4 53.2 94.7 65.0 68.0 Deep- 2017), TS2vec (Yue et al., 2022), TimesNet (Wu Learning TimesNet 61.06 77.9 42.1 91.3 61.9 67.3 ShapeNet 55.28 86.3 59.3 94.0 53.3 68.2 et al., 2022), ShapeNet (Li et al., 2021b), RL- RLPAM 66.75 89.5 55.1 90.0 67.4 74.3 PAM (Gao et al., 2022b), ShapeConv (Qu et al., ShapeConv 61.24 89.0 54.1 95.0 64.8 72.4 SBM 59.8 86.0 45.8 94.1 66.7 70.5 2024b), SBM (Wen et al., 2025a), InterpGN InterpGN 58.73 91.4 52.6 98.3 70.7 73.7 (Wen et al., 2025a); and Ensemble-based meth- Ours TimeSliver 66.9 90.9 55.2 93.6 76.1 75.6 ods. More details are provided in Section E of the Appendix. We report the predictive performance in Table 4 and observe that: (1) TimeSliver demonstrates superior performance on datasets with long sequences (length > 1000), achieving an average absolute improvement of 4.4% over the best-performing baselines (details in Appendix E); (2) it improves performance on electrical biosignals by 6.3% on average ; and (3) overall, TimeSliver delivers competitive predictive performance, staying within 2% of the best baselines across diverse applications. To further showcase TimeSliver ’s strong performance, we report its average rank, top-1, and top-3 counts compared to all baselines in Appendix E. To empirically demonstrate TimeSliver ’s ability to capture temporally disjoint yet jointly informative patterns, we construct a synthetic dataset with multiplicative interactions in disjoint segments (Appendix D.3) and evaluate its predictive performance against multiple architectures. As shown

in Appendix D.3, TimeSliver achieves performance within 1% of the baselines, confirming its effectiveness in modeling temporally disjoint interactions.', 621, 3073, 'high')
