INSERT INTO paper_text_chunks (chunk_id, paper_version_id, section_id, sequence_index, chunk_index_in_section, page_start, page_end, content_type, text_content, token_count, char_count, location_confidence)
VALUES ('paper_034_iclr_2026_proceedings_chunk_0040', 'paper_034_iclr_2026_proceedings', 'paper_034_iclr_2026_proceedings_sec_f_1', 43, 5, 23, 23, 'body_text', 'Table 12: TimeSliver vs. 16 baselines on 26 UEA datasets, evaluated using Average Rank, Top-1 Count, and Top-3 Count. Bold indicates the best result, underlined indicates the second-best. ↑ and ↓ denote that higher and lower values are better, respectively. Type Method Average Rank ↓ Top-1 Count ↑ Top-3 Count ↑ DTW_D 16.88 0 0 Distance-Based DTW_I 18.65 0 0 DTW_A 14.67 1 1 MUSE 10.50 3 6 Dictionary-Based gRSF 16.88 0 0 CIF 11.31 1 3 MrSEQL 12.88 0 0 Feature-Based ML ROCKET 9.31 4 7 TapNet 17.77 1 1 ResNet 15.38 1 1 IncTime 7.69 4 7 FCN 9.92 1 5 TS2Vec 16.88 0 0 Deep Learning TimesNet 15.92 0 0 ShapeNet 12.58 1 2 RLPAM 10.23 4 10 ShapeConv 11.19 1 4 SBM 11.15 1 3 InterpGN 7.08 3 7 Ours TimeSliver 7.00 6 11', 179, 714, 'high')
ON CONFLICT DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO paper_text_chunks (chunk_id, paper_version_id, section_id, sequence_index, chunk_index_in_section, page_start, page_end, content_type, text_content, token_count, char_count, location_confidence)
VALUES ('paper_034_iclr_2026_proceedings_chunk_0041', 'paper_034_iclr_2026_proceedings', 'paper_034_iclr_2026_proceedings_sec_f_2', 44, 0, 23, 23, 'body_text', 'To satisfy the sensitivity axiom, any change or perturbation ( δ ) to a temporal segment x = x [ t : s s t + m ] that induces a change in the predicted logit y ˆ must yield a nonzero attribution score for that c segment, i.e., y ˆ ( x ) ̸ = y ˆ ( x ) = ⇒ ϕ ̸ = 0 , c c [ x s ← x s + δ s ] s In TimeSliver , the attribution score computation (Equations 3 and 4) involves two key steps after training:

• Sensitivity of y ˆ with respect to P . We first estimate g = ∂ y ˆ c . Although gradient-based c ij ∂P ij methods typically violate sensitivity (Shrikumar et al., 2017), TimeSliver preserves it because y ˆ depends linearly on P (Equation 9). Thus, any perturbation in P that affects y ˆ c c necessarily yields g ̸ = 0 . ij • Separating positive and negative contributions. In the second step, we use σ = ij sign( w ) together with the Z Q terms in Equation 3 to isolate positively and negatively ij ki kj contributing segments via the ReLU operator. Consequently, TimeSliver satisfies sensitivity partially : ϕ + is insensitive to negatively contributing segments (they are zeroed), and ϕ − is insensitive to positively contributing segments.', 299, 1145, 'high')
ON CONFLICT DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO paper_text_chunks (chunk_id, paper_version_id, section_id, sequence_index, chunk_index_in_section, page_start, page_end, content_type, text_content, token_count, char_count, location_confidence)
VALUES ('paper_034_iclr_2026_proceedings_chunk_0042', 'paper_034_iclr_2026_proceedings', 'paper_034_iclr_2026_proceedings_sec_f_3', 45, 0, 23, 24, 'body_text', 'For a method to be symmetry-preserving, it must assign identical attribution scores ( ϕ + and ϕ − ) to two identical temporal segments in the input sequence. To illustrate that TimeSliver satisfies this property, consider a univariate time-series instance x ∈ R L × 1 containing two identical segments x = x [ t : t + m ] and x ′ = x [ t : t + m ] . As shown in Equation 3, the attribution score for a s 1 1 s 2 2 segment x primarily depends on Z and Q . Section 2.2.2 establishes that Z depends solely on the i i i one-hot encoding O (Equation 1). Because identical raw segments yield identical one-hot encodings, we have O = O ′ . Likewise, the latent representation q is obtained from a 1D CNN applied at the s s i

segment level; therefore, identical segments produce identical latent representations, i.e., q = q ′ . s s Consequently, both Z and Q are identical for the two segments. By Equation 3, this yields identical attribution scores ζ + and ζ − , and therefore identical final attributions ϕ + and ϕ − .

