Figure 2: HD with CHARM . The input is an attributed graph (shown in the bottom left). First f mp obtains refined node / token representations via msg-passing. Next, f aggregates these if response pool level predictions are required. Finally, a projection head, f , outputs the detection score. pred

from the aforementioned sparsification and offering a compelling advantage in terms of computational complexity. In particular, we structure f as: f = f ◦ f ◦ f (see Figure 2), where: pred pool mp

• f stacks learnable message-passing layers to compute updated token representations from mp the input graph G ; • f aggregates token representations into a single graph representation (e.g., via averaging pool or summation), or acts as the identity for token-wise detection; • f applies dense layers to compute graph- or token-wise hallucination predictions. pred

Starting from the original node (token) features X , each layer in f calculates and updates hidden V mp node representations by aggregating them (possibly, non-linearly) along the connectivity defined by the attention scores as per Equation (1). That is, the t -th layer updates token i ’s embedding as: (cid:16) (cid:0) (cid:1)(cid:17) h ( t +1) = up h ( t ) , msg h ( t ) ,h ( t ) ,x τ ,p (2) i t i t i j E, ( i,j ) i,j j : ( i,j ) ∈ E where: is a permutation invariant 2 aggregator such as sum, average, or max; x τ corresponds E, ( i,j ) to the features of edge ( i,j ) in X τ ; h (0) = x are the initial node features; p is a one-hot vector E i V,i i,j indicating whether edge ( i,j ) connects prompt to response or response to response tokens. Functions up , msg are parameterised as Multi-Layer Perceptrons (MLPs) running on the concatenation of t t their arguments. We refer to our overall approach as CHARM , a mnemonic formula for “Catching HAllucinated Responses via (learnable) Message-passing”.', 853, 3565, 'high')
