INSERT INTO paper_text_chunks (chunk_id, paper_version_id, section_id, sequence_index, chunk_index_in_section, page_start, page_end, content_type, text_content, token_count, char_count, location_confidence)
VALUES ('paper_035_iclr_2026_proceedings_chunk_0014', 'paper_035_iclr_2026_proceedings', 'paper_035_iclr_2026_proceedings_sec_5_1', 14, 1, 8, 8, 'body_text', 'Results, reported in Table 1, show our approach consistently outperforms all baselines across both datasets and metrics in the att.-only configuration. The significant margins over Lookback Lens , Neigh-Avg(N) and Neigh-Avg(E) underscore the benefits of an expressive, learnable graph-based method over attention-based heuristics. This pure attention configuration also surpasses all activation-based Act -* probes, this contributing to answer positively to Q1 . We interestingly report that including activations from an intermediate layer into CHARM reveals detrimental. We hypothesise that, on these contextual benchmarks, attention features alone carry most of the relevant predictive signal — an expressive enough model like ours can leverage it at best and struggle to find additional complementary signals on activations, which could, instead lead to fit spurious correlations.', 159, 884, 'high')
ON CONFLICT DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO paper_text_chunks (chunk_id, paper_version_id, section_id, sequence_index, chunk_index_in_section, page_start, page_end, content_type, text_content, token_count, char_count, location_confidence)
VALUES ('paper_035_iclr_2026_proceedings_chunk_0015', 'paper_035_iclr_2026_proceedings', 'paper_035_iclr_2026_proceedings_sec_5_2', 15, 0, 8, 8, 'body_text', 'Datasets. We next evaluate CHARM at the coarser response-level HD on three datasets: Movies (Orgad et al., 2025), WinoBias (Zhao et al., 2018), and Math (Sun et al., 2024). Unlike NQ and CNN, these benchmarks address failure modes different than contextual grounding, namely: factual knowledge recall (Movies), intrinsic bias in coreference resolution (WinoBias), and arithmetic reasoning (Math). This allows to assess generalisation across fundamentally different hallucination types. The relative role of attention thereon is not obviously clear, but we hypothesise they can provide informative signals, e.g. by capturing systematic biases in attention to demographic cues or by reflecting unusual patterns in intermediate calculations steps. Exploring their interplay with other computational traces is thus a insightful direction. For these experiments we derive text generations and hallucination annotations following the procedure in (Orgad et al., 2025) and, consistently with this work, we target a different LLM, Mistral-7B-instruct . More dataset details are in Appendix B.2.

Method comparisons. As for CHARM and its non-learnable counterparts (iv), we set component f to average . We compare our method to Act -*’s probing activations in notoriously relevant pool token positions, e.g., the last token of the prompt, or the last of the response (Orgad et al., 2025) (see Appendix C.4). Here, we compare against the response-level attention-based LLM-Check (Sriramanan et al., 2024) ( LLM-Chk -*) and the spectral-method proposed in (Binkowski et al., 2025) ( LapEig ). We also run an enhanced counterpart of ( LLM-Chk++ -*) whereby per-head scores are considered as inputs to logistic regression, rather than being averaged (Equation (4)).

Results are re- Table 2: Test AUROC and AUPR (%) for Movies, Winobias, Math (responseported in Table 2. lv, higher is better). Bold : best, Underlined: runner-up. Overall, CHARM attains the best perfor- Movies Winobias Math mance across these Method AUROC AUPR AUROC AUPR AUROC AUPR benchmarks, with particularly notable Probas 58.6 81.6 64.5 20.0 54.5 57.4 gaps in Math. To- Act -24 77.0 90.4 76.6 37.8 77.7 77.5 gether with the above, Act -28 77.0 90.4 73.9 34.3 78.1 77.8 Act -32 76.3 90.2 72.7 35.3 76.6 77.9 these results answer LLM-Chk -24 47.5 74.6 38.9 10.9 64.5 68.2 positively to Q1 and LLM-Chk -28 51.1 76.7 41.6 11.4 65.5 69.2 Q2 . Interestingly, we LLM-Chk -32 61.5 82.1 41.6 11.3 64.0 67.6 observe a markedly LLM-Chk++ -24 66.3 84.5 64.6 20.5 67.3 69.1 different behaviour LLM-Chk++ -28 67.8 86.3 64.8 21.0 67.0 70.6 LLM-Chk++ -32 73.0 88.8 67.2 24.1 68.6 72.5 than in the previously LapEig 72.9 88.4 74.1 33.3 73.6 76.3 considered contex- Neigh-Avg(N) 78.6 91.2 63.8 23.0 77.4 79.2 tual HD datasets. Neigh-Avg(E) 54.9 78.5 65.8 21.9 76.7 78.3 Other than Movies s CHARM (att) 80.3 ± 0.2 92.0 ± 0.1 70.4 ± 0.7 29.1 ± 1.0 76.5 ± 1.1 79.7 ± 0.5 — where both our r u o CHARM (att+act-24) 79.7 ± 0.3 91.8 ± 0.2 77.8 ± 0.4 39.8 ± 1.3 80.8 ± 0.7 83.1 ± 0.7 configurations work equally well — on both Winobias and Math, activation-based features work in synergy with attention-based ones. Instead of leading to fit spurious correlations as hypothesised for NQ and CNN (Section 5.1), they contribute to strongly boost performance over the att.-only CHARM , and over all considered att.- and act.-based methods. This is particularly pronounced on Winobias — there, CHARM (att.) is surpassed by Act -*’s, but the inclusion of activations leads it (att. & act.-24) to significantly outperform them', 745, 3553, 'high')
ON CONFLICT DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO paper_text_chunks (chunk_id, paper_version_id, section_id, sequence_index, chunk_index_in_section, page_start, page_end, content_type, text_content, token_count, char_count, location_confidence)
VALUES ('paper_035_iclr_2026_proceedings_chunk_0016', 'paper_035_iclr_2026_proceedings', 'paper_035_iclr_2026_proceedings_sec_5_2', 16, 1, 9, 9, 'body_text', 'both. Overall these datasets provide cases leading to a positive answer to Q5 . We last note that Math is the only dataset where Act − 24 outperformed by other variants, namely Act -32. We thus also ran CHARM (att+act-32), which scored Test AUROC of 81 . 7 ± 0 . 2 , and AUPR of 83 . 8 ± 0 . 3 .', 83, 295, 'high')
