Figure 8: The test accuracy of Q-learning on four data splits after fine-tuning the SFT model on D . All accuracies are evaluated with greedy decoding. RL - Train

RL algorithms introduced in Section 2: PG and Q-learning, whose training curves are presented in Figures 7 and 8, respectively. All accuracies are evaluated using greedy decoding. FromFigure7, weobservetheopposingeffectsofKLregularizationon D and D . Train2Test Test2Train PG without KL regularization ( λ = 0 ) and less regularized PG ( λ = 0 . 0001 ) achieve significantly higher accuracy on D . Stronger KL regularization hinders the model’s ability to learn new Test2Train pairs, which aligns with Takeaway 4 : KL regularization reduces training accuracy. Conversely, PG without KL regularization ( λ = 0 ) tends to overfit the training data and exhibits continual forgetting of previous knowledge learned during SFT. Results on D further demonstrate that overly Test2Test strong KL regularization can hinder PG’s improvement. Among all settings, λ = 10 − 4 achieves the best balance, indicating that a well-chosen KL weight can improve generalization with minimal sacrifice in training accuracy. ComparedtoPGandtheperformanceobservedinSection5, Q-learningexhibitsslowerconvergence in this setting. One possible explanation is that the initial model performs poorly on the new training pairs, generating more failure cases and causing stronger “re-instantiation.”', 878, 3433, 'high')
