VALUES ('paper_036_ev_005', 'assessment_ai_paper_036_iclr_2026_proceedings', 'paper_036_iclr_2026_proceedings_chunk_0040', NULL, 23, NULL, 'text', 'paper_036_iclr_2026_proceedings_chunk_0040', 'quote', 'Beyond the setupin Section 2, weconduct additional experiments to compareRL methods with SFT. The graph construction and initial SFT stage remain unchanged. After SFT, we split all reachable pairs into an RL training set D and an RL test set D . This yields four intersections: RL - Train RL - Test D := D ∩ D , D := D ∩ D , D := D ∩ Train2Train Train RL - Train Train2Test Train RL - Test Test2Train Test D , and D := D ∩ D . RL - Train Test2Test Test RL - Test During the RL process, the model generates paths for pairs in D and receives reward signals. RL - Train The main difference between this setup and that in Section 2 is that the RL training set now contains new pairs that were unseen during SFT ( D ). Therefore, the initial model is not perfect on Test2Train these new training pairs. Additionally, some pairs from the SFT training set are not used for RL training ( D ), which allows us', 'high', 'paper_036_iclr_2026_proceedings_sec_g_2', NULL, FALSE)
ON CONFLICT DO NOTHING;

