Figure 10: Top and bottom ranked samples by DataMIL for each of the real-world tasks

Figure 10 visualizes the highest and lowest ranked datapoints identified by DataMIL for real-world tasks. As discussed in the main text, these examples often appear visually similar, aligning with findings in computer vision (Ilyas et al., 2022; Feldman & Zhang, 2020) where data points that look alike but carry different labels can mislead the model. In robotics, this may occur when visually similar observations correspond to different actions, thereby confusing the policy. How and why these fine-grained differences affect data selection, and ultimately, the policy performance, is an interesting direction for future work.', 218, 1089, 'high')
ON CONFLICT DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO paper_text_chunks (chunk_id, paper_version_id, section_id, sequence_index, chunk_index_in_section, page_start, page_end, content_type, text_content, token_count, char_count, location_confidence)
VALUES ('paper_037_iclr_2026_proceedings_chunk_0031', 'paper_037_iclr_2026_proceedings', 'paper_037_iclr_2026_proceedings_sec_b', 33, 0, 16, 16, 'body_text', 'In this section, we describe the datamodeling framework in more detail. In particular, we first provide the formal version of Informal Definition 1, then describe the estimators that we use to construct datamodels in this work, namely the regression estimator and the metagradient-based estimator.', 57, 297, 'high')
ON CONFLICT DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO paper_text_chunks (chunk_id, paper_version_id, section_id, sequence_index, chunk_index_in_section, page_start, page_end, content_type, text_content, token_count, char_count, location_confidence)
VALUES ('paper_037_iclr_2026_proceedings_chunk_0032', 'paper_037_iclr_2026_proceedings', 'paper_037_iclr_2026_proceedings_sec_b_1', 34, 0, 17, 17, 'body_text', 'The goal of datamodeling is to construct a function f ˆ that can predict the performance of a learning algorithm on any given subset of the data ′ without actually training a policy on that A D ⊂ D subset. Let be a prior dataset of imitation-learning data of size N = , and let us represent D N |D| any subset of as a binary vector w 0 , 1 where w = 1 if the i -th training sample is in i D ∈ { } the subset and w = 0 otherwise. Let a learning algorithm be a function that takes as input a i A dataset (represented as a binary vector w ) and outputs a policy ( w ) . The datamodeling problem A is to construct a function f ˆ that can predict the performance of ( w ) when trained on any given A subset of the data without actually training a policy on that subset. More formally, we aim to find a function f ˆ minimizing the following loss: (cid:20)(cid:16) (cid:17) (cid:21) E ( ( w )) f ˆ ( w ) , (6) w ∼ Bernoulli ( 1 ) N M A − where is the target metric and w Bernoulli ( 1 ) N is a random binary vector of length N . M ∼ 2 Recall from the main text that we are particularly interested in datamodels f ˆ that are additive in the training dataset—in terms of our formalization, we are interested in functions f ˆ of the form f ˆ ( w ) = w ⊤ β. (7) for some vector β R N . An estimation method for datamodels is thus just a method for finding a ∈ good estimate of the vector β . We refer the reader to Ilyas et al. (2022) for a more detailed discussion of datamodeling.', 410, 1471, 'high')
ON CONFLICT DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO paper_text_chunks (chunk_id, paper_version_id, section_id, sequence_index, chunk_index_in_section, page_start, page_end, content_type, text_content, token_count, char_count, location_confidence)
VALUES ('paper_037_iclr_2026_proceedings_chunk_0033', 'paper_037_iclr_2026_proceedings', 'paper_037_iclr_2026_proceedings_sec_b_2', 35, 0, 17, 17, 'body_text', 'The regression estimator is a simple yet effective method for estimating the vector β that treats datamodeling as a supervised learning problem. In particular, the regression estimator first samples a set of m binary vectors w ,..., w Bernoulli ( p ) N where p is the probability of inclusion; for 1 m ∼ each of these binary vectors, it trains a policy ( w ) on the subset of the data indexed by w , and i i A evaluates its performance using the target metric . It then fits a linear model to the performance M of these policies on the sampled binary vectors, i.e., it solves m 1 (cid:88) (cid:0) ⊤ (cid:1) 2 min ( ( w )) w β , (8) β ∈ R N m M A i − i i =1 and uses the resulting vector β ˆ as the parameters of the datamodel f ˆ ( w ) = w ⊤ β ˆ . The cost of building this estimator is high, since it requires training a policy for each of the m binary vectors, but Ilyas et al. (2022) shows that the estimator can be very accurate, and indeed identifies highly influential subsets of the prior dataset. Pseudocode for the regression estimator is provided in Algorithm 1.', 280, 1072, 'high')
ON CONFLICT DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO paper_text_chunks (chunk_id, paper_version_id, section_id, sequence_index, chunk_index_in_section, page_start, page_end, content_type, text_content, token_count, char_count, location_confidence)
VALUES ('paper_037_iclr_2026_proceedings_chunk_0034', 'paper_037_iclr_2026_proceedings', 'paper_037_iclr_2026_proceedings_sec_b_3', 36, 0, 17, 18, 'body_text', 'The metagradient-based estimator is a more sophisticated method for estimating the vector β that avoids the high cost of training a policy for each binary vector. Instead, the metagradient-based estimator operates by leveraging a classical statistical tool called the influence function (Hampel, 1974). Intuitively, the metagradient-based estimator proceeds as follows. First, instead of thinking of training datasets as binary vectors w 0 , 1 N , we think of them as real-valued vectors w N ∈ { } ∈ [0 , 1] , where each coordinate w corresponds to the importance weight placed on the i -th training i sample in the dataset. Concretely, if w = 0 , then the i -th training sample is not used in the training i set, and if w = 1 , then the i -th training sample is used in the training set with full weight; if i 0 < w < 1 , then the i -th training sample is used in the training set but its loss is scaled by z . i i Observe that this parameterization is equivalent to the binary parameterization for w 0 , 1 , but i ∈ { } gives us a continuous way to represent the training set. Once we have this continuous parameterization, we can write the first-order approximation to ( ( w )) as, M A ( ( w )) ( ( w )) + ( ( w )) ⊤ ( w w ) , (9) 0 0 0 M A ≈ M A ∇M A −

