INSERT INTO paper_text_chunks (chunk_id, paper_version_id, section_id, sequence_index, chunk_index_in_section, page_start, page_end, content_type, text_content, token_count, char_count, location_confidence)
VALUES ('paper_037_iclr_2026_proceedings_chunk_0035', 'paper_037_iclr_2026_proceedings', 'paper_037_iclr_2026_proceedings_sec_b_3', 37, 1, 18, 19, 'body_text', 'where w is the vector of all ones. The gradient ( ( w )) is known as the influence function , 0 0 ∇M A and gives us a linear approximation to the loss in Equation 6. That is, if we could compute the influence function exactly, we could use it as a datamodel directly, i.e., f ˆ ( w ) = ( ( w )) + ( ( w )) ⊤ ( w w ) . (10) 0 0 0 M A ∇M A − Traditionally, the influence function is notoriously hard to compute, and so prior work on data attribution has focused on approximating it (Koh & Liang, 2017; Park et al., 2023; Bae et al., 2024). However, recent work has shown how to compute it exactly and efficiently (Engstrom et al., 2025) and how to use this exact influence function as a datamodel estimator (Ilyas & Engstrom, 2025). Observe that in order for the metagradient-based estimator to be valid, the function ( ( w )) must M A be differentiable with respect to w . For this to be satisfied, it is sufficient for (a) the target metric to be differentiable with respect to the policy ( w ) , and (b) the policy ( w ) to be trained via M A A an iterative algorithm composed of elementary differentiable operations (which is almost all of the popular off-the-shelf learning algorithms). Gradient descent on training data. To operationalize the metagradient-based estimator, we use the Metagradient Descent (MGD) algorithm (Engstrom et al., 2025), to compute influence scores and iteratively refine the dataset. The algorithm views data selection as an optimization problem over the selected data counts c 0 , 1 N , where c = 1 indicates inclusion of the i -th sample. i ∈ { } Given a count vector c , let denote the policy obtained by training on the dataset defined by c , c A and let ( ) denote its evaluation on the target metric. MGD introduces a differentiable surrogate c ′ ( w ) M , where A w R N perturbs the per-sample weights during training only at a certain iteration A c ∈ k , while keeping the rest of the training as is. By construction, setting w = 0 recovers the original training procedure, i.e., ′ ( 0 ) = . c c A A We can then compute the influence/metagradient as, (cid:12) g = ( ′ ( w )) (cid:12) , (11) w c (cid:12) ∇ M A w = 0 whichmeasureshowaninfinitesimalupweightingofeachtrainingexampleatthe k thiterationwould affect the downstream performance. The sign of g thus reveals whether increasing or decreasing i the weight of sample i would improve . M To update the dataset, MGD applies stochastic coordinate updates: a random mask m Bernoulli ( p ) N selects a subset of coordinates to update, and the counts are modified as ∼ c c sign( g ) m , (12) ← − ⊙ where denotes elementwise multiplication. This step is analogous to performing gradient descent ⊙ over training samples with p as the learning rate . Repeating this process iteratively over T steps

