INSERT INTO paper_text_chunks (chunk_id, paper_version_id, section_id, sequence_index, chunk_index_in_section, page_start, page_end, content_type, text_content, token_count, char_count, location_confidence)
VALUES ('paper_038_iclr_2026_proceedings_chunk_0026', 'paper_038_iclr_2026_proceedings', 'paper_038_iclr_2026_proceedings_sec_a_1', 26, 0, 16, 17, 'body_text', 'We summarize the main notations in the paper in Table 5, and summarize the meteorological variables studied in this work in Table 6.

Table 5: Summary of main notations.

Symbol Description Dimension I Low-resolution input field R H × W I High-resolution target field R H ′ × W ′ HR I Frequency-aware representation R d × H × W f P Frequency-aware projection P : I (cid:55)→ I ψ ψ f G Geographical implicit neural representation R d × H × W M Frequency-aware geography-informed representation R d × H × W P Data patch R h × w C Discrete cosine transformed data patch R N × N N DCT block size scalar ( x,y ) Spatial coordinates R 2 ( u,v ) Frequency indices R 2 B DCT basis R N × N u,v P Frequency-specific response R N × N u,v ( λ,ϕ,h ) Latitude, longitude, and elevation coordinate R 3 M 3D geographic manifold S 2 × R G Implicit neural mapping M → R d PE Positional encoding PE : R 3 → R d 0 NN Neural network NN : R d 0 → R d Y m Spherical harmonic basis S 2 ℓ Y Truncated spherical harmonics encoding R ( L +1) 2 L T Terrain vector R 3 T (cid:98) Terrain-differential encoding R 3 Ψ Learnable layer for terrain-differential encoding Ψ : R 3 → R ( L +1) 2 f Sinusoidal activation function f : R d → R d k k

Table 6: Summary of meteorological variables.

Abbreviation Full Name Source Unit T2m 2m temperature ERA5, CERRA K Z500 geopotential 500hPa ERA5 m 2 s − 2 T850 temperature 850hPa ERA5 K 10u 10m u component of wind CERRA ms − 1 10v 10m v component of wind CERRA ms − 1 Rh2m 2m relative humidity CERRA % Sp surface pressure CERRA Pa LST land surface temperature MODIS K SKT skin temperature ERA5-Land K', 422, 1611, 'high')
ON CONFLICT DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO paper_text_chunks (chunk_id, paper_version_id, section_id, sequence_index, chunk_index_in_section, page_start, page_end, content_type, text_content, token_count, char_count, location_confidence)
VALUES ('paper_038_iclr_2026_proceedings_chunk_0027', 'paper_038_iclr_2026_proceedings', 'paper_038_iclr_2026_proceedings_sec_a_2_1', 27, 0, 17, 17, 'body_text', 'ERA5 Reanalysis. ERA5 Reanalysis (Hersbach et al., 2020) is maintained by the European Center for Medium-Range Weather Forecasting (ECMWF). ERA5 is a reanalysis that combines diverse observational data with forecasts from the state-of-the-art Integrated Forecasting System (IFS). It provides the estimation of the state of the atmosphere and land-surface variables at any given time. In its raw format, ERA5 provides hourly data from 1979 to the present on a 0 . 25 ◦ global grid, covering a range of atmospheric variables at 37 pressure levels in addition to surface conditions. We preprocess the 2m-temperature (T2m), geopotential at 500hPa (Z500), and temperature at 850hPa (T850) in ERA5 according to the ClimateLearn benchmark into 5 . 625 ◦ (32 × 64) and 2 . 8125 ◦ (64 × 128) global grids with hourly updates for climate downscaling, we use the years 1981-2015 for training, the year 2016 for validation, and 2017-2018 for testing. PRISM. PRISM (PRISM Climate Group, 2025) is a dataset of observed atmospheric variables, including precipitation and temperature, covering the conterminous United States at varying spatial and temporal resolutions from 1895 to the present. At its highest publicly available resolution, PRISM provides daily data on a 4 × 4 km grid, corresponding to a matrix of 621 × 1405 cells. Following the ClimateLearn benchmark, we process daily max T2m in PRISM to 0 . 75 ◦ . We crop the corresponding regions in ERA5 to align with PRISM when downscaling. The training period is from 1981 to 2015, validation is in 2016, and the testing period is from 2017 to 2018. CERRA. Copernicus European Regional Reanalysis for Europe (CERRA) is a pan-European regional reanalysis produced by C3S/ECMWF at 5.5 km horizontal resolution, with the core production spanning September 1984 to June 2021. For single-variable experiments (Table 1 and Table 3b), we specifically employ the 2-m air temperature (t2m) field available as a single-level variable via the C3S Climate Data Store. For our experiments, we use bilinear interpolation to process the native 5.5 km data ( 1069 × 1069 ) to grids of 5.5 ( 1068 × 1068 ), 11 ( 534 × 534 ), 22 ( 267 × 267 ), and 44 ( 133 × 133 ) km with 3-hour updates to define multiple evaluation settings, we use 2010-2017 for training, 2018-2021 for validation and testing. For multi-variable experiments (Table 2), we employ the 2m-temperature (T2m), 10m-u-component-of-wind (10u), 10m-v-component-of-wind (10v), 2m-relative-humidity (Rh2m), and surface-presssure (Sp). The native 5.5 km data are further preprocessed with bilinear interpolation to grids of 11 ( 534 × 534 ), 22 ( 267 × 267 ) km with 3-hour updates for the 2 × super-resolution. The training set is from 2010 to 2017, and the val/test sets are from 2018 to 2021, leading to a large-scale multi-variable downscaling dataset of roughly 200GB. MODIS. Moderate Resolution Imaging Spectroradiometer (MODIS) is a satellite from NASA, whose data are used to create a number of operational products. To assess the model’s generalization ability on this data source, we construct a new dataset aiming at downscaling the daily skin temperature (SKT) from ERA5-Land to the MODIS land surface temperature (LST). Specifically, we download both datasets over Switzerland, which is a very challenging region with complex atmospheric processes, due to its complex alpine topography. We preprocess ERA5-Land to 11 km resolution ( 21 × 46 ), and MODIS to roughly 1.1 km resolution ( 210 × 460 ) via bilinear interpolation, leading to a challenging setting of × 10 downscaling. The training set is from 2018 to 2022, and the testing set is from 2023.', 774, 3648, 'high')
ON CONFLICT DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO paper_text_chunks (chunk_id, paper_version_id, section_id, sequence_index, chunk_index_in_section, page_start, page_end, content_type, text_content, token_count, char_count, location_confidence)
VALUES ('paper_038_iclr_2026_proceedings_chunk_0028', 'paper_038_iclr_2026_proceedings', 'paper_038_iclr_2026_proceedings_sec_a_2_2', 28, 0, 18, 18, 'body_text', 'We normalize both the input and target image I ∈ R H × W to stabilize model training:

I ( x,y ) − µ I ˜ ( x,y ) = , ( x,y ) ∈ { 1 ,...,H } × { 1 ,...,W } , (12) σ + ε where µ and σ are the mean and standard deviation (computed over the training set), and ε > 0 ensures numerical stability. In addition, we add a constant offset (1.0) to the frequency-aware representation I , to prevent near-zero entries in I from nullifying the contribution of the geographical f f embedding during the per-pixel product.', 136, 507, 'high')
