INSERT INTO paper_text_chunks (chunk_id, paper_version_id, section_id, sequence_index, chunk_index_in_section, page_start, page_end, content_type, text_content, token_count, char_count, location_confidence)
VALUES ('paper_039_iclr_2026_proceedings_chunk_0006', 'paper_039_iclr_2026_proceedings', 'paper_039_iclr_2026_proceedings_sec_3', 6, 1, 4, 4, 'body_text', 'K V ( M ) = (ˆ p [ k ] ( M ) a ) 2 . (7) K ij ij n ( n 1) ↘ ↘ k " =1 ( v i ,v " j ) k ↑ S In practice, p ˆ [ k ] ( M ) may exceed one or fall below zero due to computational or measurement errors. ij In such cases, we truncate the estimated p ˆ [ k ] ( M ) to ensure it lies within the interval [0 , 1] . Given a ij set of candidate models , we select the optimal model M which minimizes V ( M ) . The detailed V K M algorithm for the proposed K-fold cross-validation is provided in the Appendix Algorithm 1. Computational cost. To evaluate a set of candidate hyperparameters on an n -node network |M| using K -fold cross-validation, the total computational complexity of our CV-imputation method is O ( ( KC ( n )+ n 2 )) , where C ( n ) denotes the cost of the specific graphon estimator (e.g., estim estim |M|· NS, ICE). The detailed analysis can be found in Section S.8. Existing graphon estimation method usually have C greater than n 2 . So the cost is mainly dominated by the graphon estimation estim method itself rather than by CV-imputation itself. In contrast, the competing ECV method has complexity O ( ( KC ( n ) + KT ( n ))) where T ( n ) is the matrix completion complexity. estim mc mc |M| · The critical difference between our CV-imputation and ECV is the additional overhead per fold: Our method adds a simple O ( n 2 ) cost for imputation and transformation. ECV adds a much larger O ( T ( n )) cost for matrix completion, which is typically O ( n 3 ) for a full SVD. Thus our method mc is more computationally efficient than ECV. For very large networks, a practical way to scale CVimputation is to combine it with network subsampling: (1) extract a structurally representative subgraph using network sampling methods (e.g., Metropolis-Hastings random walk Hu and Lau (2013), curvature-based sampling Wu et al. (2023)), (2) apply CV-imputation on this subgraph to tune hyperparameters, and (3) fit the full network using the selected hyperparameter.', 504, 1970, 'high')
ON CONFLICT DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO paper_text_chunks (chunk_id, paper_version_id, section_id, sequence_index, chunk_index_in_section, page_start, page_end, content_type, text_content, token_count, char_count, location_confidence)
