Theorem 1. As n and K , ↓ ⇑ ↓ ⇑ 1 1 1 V ( M ) L ( M ) ! = O (8) K ↘ ↘ p n ⇓ K (1+ ω ) / 2 ⇓ K ω $ & uniformly, where ! = 2 p (1 p ) , and denotes the operator that returns the largest n ( n 1) i<j ij ↘ ij ⇓ of two values. ↓ ’ Theorem 1 suggests that the validation score V ( M ) is a consistent estimator of L ( M ) + ! with an K error rate of 1 1 1 . Note that ! does not depend on M , so the validation score function n ⇓ K (1+ ω ) / 2 ⇓ K ω V ( ) and the loss function L ( ) are asymptotically parallel in probability. Thus, the probability K · · that the minimizer of V ( M ) approximately minimizes L ( M ) is high within a neighborhood of M . K 0 Theorem 1 essentially establishes the consistency of the CV-imputation score, ensuring that the score for each candidate model is close to its true loss up to a constant. The proof of Theorem 1 is provided in the appendix. It is worth noting that Condition 1 specifies that the maximum K -fold optimism bias is bounded by a polynomial rate of K ω . The polynomial order ε is determined by the complexity of the ↓ underlying graphon model and the efficiency of the estimation methods. For instance, in the case of an Erd˝ os–Rényi model (Erdos and Renyi, 1959) where f ( µ ,µ ) p , we have ε = 1 when using i j ⇔ the simple averaging estimator and letting K n . Unlike many assumptions that are not verifiable, Q ( M ) can be verified computationally, as both ↖ P ˆ ( M A ) and P ˆ ( M ) are accessible from the data. K k | In Figure S.3 in the Appendix, the validation of Condition 1 is provided. Further discussion of Condition 1 can be found in Section S.10.', 766, 2561, 'high')
