INSERT INTO paper_text_chunks (chunk_id, paper_version_id, section_id, sequence_index, chunk_index_in_section, page_start, page_end, content_type, text_content, token_count, char_count, location_confidence)
VALUES ('paper_042_iclr_2026_proceedings_chunk_0013', 'paper_042_iclr_2026_proceedings', 'paper_042_iclr_2026_proceedings_sec_5_3', 13, 0, 8, 9, 'body_text', 'In this section, we evaluate whether Ctrl-World can be used to evaluate the instruction-following ability of generalist robot policies and accurately reflect their performance rankings in the real world (Li et al., 2024). We set up our own DROID platform and randomly place two third-person cameras around the workspace. Similar to how prior works have seen DROID policies generalize to new setups (Pertsch et al., 2025), we find that Ctrl-World, pretrained solely on the open-sourced DROID dataset, can make accurate future predictions zero-shot in our newly configured scene with novel camera placements.

Policies and Tasks. We evaluate three publicly released policies, π (Black et al., 2023), π - 0 0 FAST (Pertsch et al., 2025), and π (Intelligence et al., 2025), across diverse tasks including 0 . 5 Pick-and-Place, Towel-Folding, Drawer, Wipe-Table, Close-Laptop, Pull-tissue and Stack tasks on our DROID platform. We initialize real-world and world model rollouts with the same initial observations and execute each policy, following Algorithm 1. We report instruction following rates and success rates in Figure 7 and visualize qualitative comparisons between real and imagined rollouts in Figure 6. More rollout details can be found in Appendix B.

Initial Obs 𝝅 𝟎.𝟓 Rollout in Real World 𝝅 𝟎.𝟓 Rollout in Wolrd Model

