INSERT INTO paper_text_chunks (chunk_id, paper_version_id, section_id, sequence_index, chunk_index_in_section, page_start, page_end, content_type, text_content, token_count, char_count, location_confidence)
VALUES ('paper_043_iclr_2026_proceedings_chunk_0017', 'paper_043_iclr_2026_proceedings', 'paper_043_iclr_2026_proceedings_sec_5', 19, 0, 8, 8, 'body_text', 'In this section, we evaluate the effectiveness of the CS fairness regularizer from several perspectives: (1) utility and fairness performance, (2) the tradeoff between utility and fairness, (3) prediction distributions across different sensitive groups, (4) T-SNE plots for these sensitive groups, and (5) the sensitivity of parameters in Equation (13). Our evaluation encompasses five datasets with diverse sensitive attributes, including four tabular datasets: Adult , COMPAS , ACS-I , and ACS-T , as well as one image dataset, CelebA-A . Utility performance is assessed based on accuracy and the area under the curve (AUC), while fairness performance is measured using △ Equation (2) DP and △ Equation (3). Detailed information about the datasets and baselines can be found in EO the Appendices E and F. We denote an observation drawn from the results as Obs. .', 177, 864, 'high')
ON CONFLICT DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO paper_text_chunks (chunk_id, paper_version_id, section_id, sequence_index, chunk_index_in_section, page_start, page_end, content_type, text_content, token_count, char_count, location_confidence)
VALUES ('paper_043_iclr_2026_proceedings_chunk_0018', 'paper_043_iclr_2026_proceedings', 'paper_043_iclr_2026_proceedings_sec_5_1', 20, 0, 8, 9, 'body_text', 'We conducted experiments on five datasets along with their corresponding baselines, as previously mentioned. For each dataset, we performed 10 different splits to ensure robustness in our results. We calculated the mean and standard deviation for each metric across these splits. The accuracy and fairness performance of the downstream tasks is in Table 2. Our observations are as follows: Obs. 1: CS achieves the best or near-best △ on most datasets and is competitive on △ , EO DP while maintaining high utility. Notably, CS demonstrates exceptional fairness performance on the image dataset, CelebA-A , where the disparity in the ‘Young’ and ‘Non-Young’ groups sees a △ reduction of 97 . 36% and a △ reduction of 98 . 58% . Furthermore, in the Adult and ACS-I DP EO datasets, which include gender groups, traditional methods such as DP , MMD , HSIC , and PR do not effectively optimize for EO fairness. In contrast, the proposed CS achieves significant reductions in △ by 72 . 12% and 63 . 85% , respectively, compared to MLP. EO Obs. 2: CS achieves good fairness performance with a small sacrifice in utility. Specifically, CS exhibits a decrease of less than 3 . 1% in accuracy and less than 2 . 2% in AUC. The only exception is observed with COMPAS when gender is treated as a sensitive attribute, resulting in a slightly higher accuracy loss of 3 . 6% . Notably, CS demonstrates either equivalent or improved AUC performance, with increases of 0 . 02% and 0 . 58% on Adult for the gender and race groups, respectively, as well as a 0 . 35% increase on COMPAS for the race group. Among the baselines, HSIC ranks highest in utility, achieving the best performance on ACS-I for the race group and on ACS-T for both the gender and race groups. This is followed by PR , which shows the best utility on COMPAS for both the gender and race groups, as well as on CelebA-A for the gender group.

DP MMD HSIC PR CS

Figure 4: Prediction distributions for female and male groups in the Adult dataset. The top row shows kernel density estimates of the raw predictions Y ˆ for all target labels, grouped by gender, while the bottom row shows the prediction densities for the positive class, Y ˆ = 1 , for the two gender groups. Each column corresponds to a different fairness regularizer. A larger overlap between the blue and red curves indicates better group fairness, and the reported values above each panel give the corresponding gaps in ∆ (top row) and ∆ (bottom row). DP EO', 571, 2474, 'high')
