2) X,S X S R p + q for a suitable weight function w ( t,u ) . In practice, we use the standard empirical distance covariance estimator. Givensamples { ( x ,s ) } n , definepairwisedistances a = ∥ x − x ∥ and b = ∥ s − s ∥ , i i i =1 ij i j ij i j and their double-centered versions: A = a − a ¯ − a ¯ + a ¯ , (43) ij ij i · · j ·· B = b − ¯ b − ¯ b +¯ b , (44) ij ij i · · j ·· where a ¯ and a ¯ denote row and column means, and a ¯ is the grand mean (and analogously for b ). i · · j ·· The empirical distance covariance is then: n n (cid:91) 2 1 (cid:88) (cid:88) dCov ( X,S ) = A B . (45) n 2 ij ij i =1 j =1 (cid:91) 2 (ˆ Our dCov-based regularizer penalizes dCov Y ,S ) .', 722, 2515, 'high')
