to ≈ 25% and AUC drops to ≈ 84–85% . The very small DP/EO gaps in this regime are therefore misleading—they correspond to a nearly random classifier. • Moderate bandwidths ( σ ∈ [2 , 20] ) In this regime, the classifier maintains high utility (AUC ≈ 90–91% , accuracy between 77% and 85 . 5% ). Fairness varies smoothly with σ : • ∆ generally improves when moving from too-local kernels ( σ = 2 , 5 ) to more moderate ones; EO σ = 15 achieves the smallest ∆ among the stable runs. EO • ∆ is best at σ = 2 , but this comes with noticeably lower accuracy. For σ ∈ { 10 , 15 , 20 } , both DP DP and EO are within a similar, reasonable range while utility is highest.

Overall, the results show that CS is not hypersensitive to the exact kernel bandwidth: once σ is chosen in a reasonable range, the method achieves consistently high AUC with a stable fairness–utility trade-off. In our main experiments we therefore use a moderate bandwidth (e.g., σ = 10 ) that lies in this stable region, balancing strong accuracy (AUC ≈ 91% ) with substantially reduced ∆ / ∆ . DP EO', 430, 1426, 'high')
