INSERT INTO paper_text_chunks (chunk_id, paper_version_id, section_id, sequence_index, chunk_index_in_section, page_start, page_end, content_type, text_content, token_count, char_count, location_confidence)
VALUES ('paper_043_iclr_2026_proceedings_chunk_0060', 'paper_043_iclr_2026_proceedings', 'paper_043_iclr_2026_proceedings_sec_l_3', 64, 0, 43, 44, 'body_text', 'To evaluate CS on multi-attribute fairness, we extend the Adult setting from a single sensitive attribute to an intersectional attribute combining sex and race. We construct four groups S ∈ { 0 , 1 , 2 , 3 } as White-Male, White-Female, Non-White-Male, and Non-White-Female. For each group g we define: DP = P (ˆ Y = 1 | S = g ) , EO = P (ˆ Y = 1 | Y = 1 ,S = g ) , Acc = P (ˆ Y = g g g Y | S = g ) . We then report the intersectional demographic-parity gap ∆ DP inter = max DP − g g min DP , the intersectional equal-opportunity gap ∆ EO inter = max EO − min EO , and the g g g g g g worst-group accuracy Acc = min Acc . The code snippet of these metrics are in Appendix N min g g Using the same MLP architecture and α (’lam’ in the figure) = 0 . 5 , we compare CS with three representative dependence-based regularizers (diffDP, HSIC, diffEOpp). The results are summarized below:

Figure 26: Evaluation of CS on the Adult (sex) as the sensitive attribute.

From Table 14, we observe that CS attains the smallest intersectional ∆ DP inter / ∆ EO inter while also achieving the highest worst-group accuracy, indicating that the proposed Cauchy–Schwarz regularizer remains effective even when fairness is evaluated over four intersectional subgroups rather than a single sensitive attribute. In particular, CS improves both ∆ DP inter and ∆ EO inter over HSIC and DP regularizer, and slightly improves Acc compared to all baselines. These results suggest that the tighter dependence min

Method ∆ DP inter ↓ ∆ EO inter ↓ Acc ↑ min diffDP 0 . 255 0 . 112 0 . 792 HSIC 0 . 262 0 . 152 0 . 791 diffEOpp 0 . 268 0 . 183 0 . 790 CS (ours) 0 . 241 0 . 108 0 . 803

