where s is the score of the most informative public sample identified via Equation (1). k ∗ ( t ) Custom Voting (Image-based). Image-based curation’s deterministic nearest-neighbor structure ( s ( x ) = max cos( ϕ ( x ) ,ϕ ( t )) ) enables reverse-engineering: for each public sample, identify which t target t ∗ = argmin | s ( x ) − sim ( ϕ ( x ) ,ϕ ( t )) | wasresponsible, increment v ( t ∗ ) (positiveevidence), t vote and decrement votes for all t where sim ( ϕ ( x ) ,ϕ ( t )) > s ( x ) (negative evidence, i.e., if t had been present, s ( x ) would be higher). The membership inference scores are the number of votes v . vote Least Squares (TRAK). TRAK-based curation computes scores as s ( x ) = 1 (cid:80) Φ( x ) ⊤ G . |T| t t Because the score is a linear combination of per-target contributions, we can directly solve for the unknown membership mask that best explains the observed scores. We denote m ∈ { 0 , 1 / |T |} |T| the masked mean operator. To recover membership signals, we then solve minimize ∥ Φ( x ) ⊤ G m − s ∥ 2 . (3) t 2 m ∈ R n The membership scores are the optimal weights v = m . App. B.4.2 further details this. lstsq', 451, 1610, 'high')
