INSERT INTO paper_text_chunks (chunk_id, paper_version_id, section_id, sequence_index, chunk_index_in_section, page_start, page_end, content_type, text_content, token_count, char_count, location_confidence)
VALUES ('paper_044_iclr_2026_proceedings_chunk_0011', 'paper_044_iclr_2026_proceedings', 'paper_044_iclr_2026_proceedings_sec_3_4', 11, 0, 5, 6, 'body_text', 'Assessingtheprivacyrisksofthecuratedpublicsubsetissignificantlymorechallengingthanattacking the curation scores. This is because the scores yield a fine-grained ranking of the public samples whereas the curated dataset itself can be considered as a binary mask that only indicates for each public data point in the pool D whether it was included into the curated set D ˜ . LiRA for Subsets. While the original LiRA is designed to operate on continuous output logits, we need our attack to operate on binary selection observations. Using the shadow sets from Section 3.2, we binarize the curation outputs to top- k masks { s ∈ { 0 , 1 } N } m representing which public i j =1 samples were selected. In a naïve setup, we directly model these binary outcomes—whether each public sample was selected (1) or not (0) in each shadow curation—using Bernoulli distributions. For each t ∈ T we compute µ ∈ R N as the average of s for every t ∈ T and µ ∈ R N where t ∈ / T . These are the in i i out i frequencies of public samples being in the top set depending on whether t was part of the curation target. Using the k ∗ filtering from Section 3.2, we select for each target t the pool sample x that t is most indicative of t ’s presence, i.e., shows the highest difference between µ and µ . We in ,t out ,t compute the membership inference score looking only the binary signal of whether x is present or t not as the log-likelihood ratio for the Bernoulli distribution (cid:18) P ( x | µ ) (cid:19) (cid:32) µ x v (1 − µ ) 1 − x v (cid:33) v ( t )log v in ,t = log in ,t in ,t . (4) BinaryLiRA P ( x | µ ) µ x v (1 − µ ) 1 − x v v out ,t out ,t out ,t

