VALUES ('paper_044_iclr_2026_proceedings_chunk_0045', 'paper_044_iclr_2026_proceedings', 'paper_044_iclr_2026_proceedings_sec_c_2', 45, 0, 23, 24, 'body_text', 'Image-based curation scores each pool sample x ∈ D based on its maximum similarity to any target sample: s ( x ) = max cos( ϕ ( x ) ,ϕ ( t )) (21) t ∈T

where ϕ ( · ) is the CLIP embedding function. This creates a one-to-many correspondence mapping between targets and pool samples. The key insight: When a target t ∗ is included in the victim’s subset T , all pool samples for which victim t ∗ is the nearest neighbor will receive higher scores and may cross the selection threshold.

We note that by default most target samples do not affect any target samples, as Figure 2 shows. But, we assume the adversary can inject samples with embeddings X = { x ,...,x } ⊂ R d into the 1 M pool to probe membership.

