INSERT INTO paper_text_chunks (chunk_id, paper_version_id, section_id, sequence_index, chunk_index_in_section, page_start, page_end, content_type, text_content, token_count, char_count, location_confidence)
VALUES ('paper_044_iclr_2026_proceedings_chunk_0047', 'paper_044_iclr_2026_proceedings', 'paper_044_iclr_2026_proceedings_sec_c_3', 47, 0, 24, 25, 'body_text', 'The TRAK system computes influence scores s = 1 (cid:80) Φ T G where Φ are the projected pool |T| t ∈T t features and G are target gradients. However, when the target uses a secret subset T ⊆ T , the t Sel. actual scores become: 1 (cid:88) s = Φ T G (26) T Sel. |T | t Sel. t ∈T Sel. We assume the adversary can inject samples with gradients C = { c ,...,c } ⊂ R d into the pool to 1 M probe membership. Obviously, gradients cannot be chosen arbitrarily. Instead, we obtain these as gradients of target samples with modified captions. When a contrastive gradient c is appended to the pool matrix X , the Sherman-Morrison formula i gives its score as:

c ⊤ G − 1 y z = q · i (27) new new 1 + c ⊤ G − 1 c i i

where G − 1 = ( X ⊤ X + λI ) − 1 is the regularized inverse Gram matrix and y = 1 (cid:80) y is the m s ∈ S s secret average. The key insight is that including target y in the secret subset creates a change in scores of the j samples: q c ⊤ G − 1 y ∆ ( c ) = new · i j (28) j i m 1 + c ⊤ G − 1 c i i

This membership signal is proportional to the preconditioned inner product c ⊤ G − 1 y and inversely i j proportional to the subset size m , making smaller subsets more vulnerable. Assuming target gradients have mean µ and covariance Σ , the signal-to-noise ratio for detecting target y using contrastive c is: j i

| c ⊤ G − 1 y | SNR ( c ) = i j (29) j i (cid:112) m · c ⊤ G − 1 Σ G − 1 c i i

For each target y , the adversary selects the contrastive maximizing SNR: j | c ⊤ G − 1 y | i ∗ ( j ) = argmax i j (30) (cid:112) i ∈ [ M ] c ⊤ G − 1 Σ G − 1 c i i

The attackability score A = max SNR ( c ) quantifies how detectable each target’s membership is. j i j i he attack succeeds when the contrastive gradient crosses the curation selection threshold. Let τ be k the score of the k -th ranked pool sample. The attack is successful if:

• Under H (target not in subset): z < τ (not selected) 0 H 0 k • Under H (target in subset): z ≥ τ (selected) 1 H 1 k

This binary change in selection status provides a clear membership signal. The attack is most effective when the contrastive score under H is just below τ , requiring only a small membership signal to 0 k cross the threshold.

To maximize the sensitivity to our target samples we experiment with adding copies of the target data to the pool. These should naturally have a high utility. To imprint a measurable membership signal in the model, we need to add some additional information, though. Mislabeling the samples as Carlini & Terzis (2022) did would serve that purpose, but those samples would not get picked by the TRAK algorithm, as it specifically rejects mislabeled samples as Park et al. (2023) have shown. So instead, we add harmless and orthogonal information , i.e., we retain the original and useful caption and add a suffix, which mostly preserves the TRAK signal (see Section C.5). Figure 11 shows the measurable difference in models trained on such samples with additional text appended to the caption.', 814, 2988, 'high')
