INSERT INTO paper_text_chunks (chunk_id, paper_version_id, section_id, sequence_index, chunk_index_in_section, page_start, page_end, content_type, text_content, token_count, char_count, location_confidence)
VALUES ('paper_045_iclr_2026_proceedings_chunk_0008', 'paper_045_iclr_2026_proceedings', 'paper_045_iclr_2026_proceedings_sec_3', 8, 0, 4, 4, 'body_text', 'The previous section defined our framework at the population level. We now turn to the practical finite-sample setting, where we must achieve ( α,ε ) -distributional unlearning using only the drawn data samples introduced in Section 2. To build an analytical understanding of the finite-sample behavior, weanalyzetheprobleminanidealizedbutfoundationalsetting: univariateGaussiandistributions with known variance. This tractable setting allows us to derive closed-form sample complexity bounds, providing crucial intuition about the relative efficiency of different removal strategies. We then validate that these insights generalize empirically to more complex settings in Section 4. Inthefollowing, weintroduceandanalyzetwodeletionstrategies: arandombaselineandaselective, distance-based method. We focus on the class of distributions P := (cid:8) N ( µ,σ 2 ): µ ∈ R (cid:9) , with known σ > 0 . Given n i.i.d. samples from the unwanted distribution p ∈ P and n from the 1 1 2 retained distribution p ∈ P , and a deletion budget 0 ≤ f ≤ n , we derive high-probability bounds 2 1 on the resulting ( α,ε ) -distributional unlearning guarantees. We defer all proofs to Appendix A.', 228, 1178, 'high')
ON CONFLICT DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO paper_text_chunks (chunk_id, paper_version_id, section_id, sequence_index, chunk_index_in_section, page_start, page_end, content_type, text_content, token_count, char_count, location_confidence)
VALUES ('paper_045_iclr_2026_proceedings_chunk_0009', 'paper_045_iclr_2026_proceedings', 'paper_045_iclr_2026_proceedings_sec_3_1', 9, 0, 4, 5, 'body_text', 'We begin with a baseline deletion strategy that treats every sample equally, deleting f points chosen uniformly at random from the n samples of p . The formal procedure is as follows: 1 1 Algorithm (Random Removal). 1. Randomly select f out of the n samples of p without replacement. 1 1 2. Remove those f samples. 3. Re-fit N (ˆ µ,σ 2 ) by MLE (maximum likelihood estimation) on the remaining data. The following theorem provides a finite-sample guarantee for achieving ( α,ε ) -distributional unlearning using random removal with a deletion budget f . Proposition 3 (Random Removal) . Let p ,p ∈ P and δ ∈ (0 , 1) . We observe n samples from p 1 2 1 1 and n samples from p , and randomly remove f samples from p before fitting. With probability 2 2 1

1 − δ , the resulting MLE distribution satisfies ( α,ε ) -distributional unlearning with: (cid:32) (cid:18) (cid:19) 2 (cid:33) (cid:18) (cid:19) 1 n − f 3ln(4 /δ ) n − f α ≥ − 3 1 KL( p ∥ p ) − 1 + 1 , 2 n 1 2 2 n n 2 2 2 (cid:18) (cid:19) 2 (cid:18) (cid:19) n − f 3ln(4 /δ ) n − f ε ≤ 3 1 KL( p ∥ p ) + 1 + 1 . n 1 2 n n 2 2 2

This result shows that the effectiveness of random removal is driven by the ratio of remaining unwanted samples to retained samples n 1 − f . An interesting aspect of these bounds is the quadratic n 2 dependence on this ratio, which indicates diminishing returns: each subsequent random deletion provides progressively less of an unlearning effect. This quadratic relationship stems from the concentration of the empirical mean, whose variance scales inversely with the sample size. While conceptually simple, this method’s inefficiency arises because it treats all samples equally, failing to prioritize those that contribute most to the unwanted statistical patterns.', 437, 1754, 'high')
