500 random data points as “audit samples” on which we evaluate membership inference, and we use mislabeled data as strong canaries to simulate worst case data; the remaining 49,500 samples are always included in every model’s training data. For each method, we train 32 shadow models, ensuring that each audit sample is included in the training data of 16 models. For all defenses, we consistently adopt ResNet-18 (He et al., 2016) as the network architecture of shadow models. We report the performance of these methods across three dimensions: privacy leakage (TPR@0.1% FPR), model utility (test accuracy), and efficiency (training time). A strong defense should achieve a balanced tradeoff among privacy, utility, and efficiency. The implementation details of all discussed defenses are provided in Appendix A.', 543, 2548, 'high')
