INSERT INTO paper_text_chunks (chunk_id, paper_version_id, section_id, sequence_index, chunk_index_in_section, page_start, page_end, content_type, text_content, token_count, char_count, location_confidence)
VALUES ('paper_047_iclr_2025_proceedings_chunk_0026', 'paper_047_iclr_2025_proceedings', 'paper_047_iclr_2025_proceedings_sec_a_2', 26, 0, 14, 15, 'body_text', 'Intuitively, high agreement fails to constrain the squared correlation ρ 2 because predictors without any signal, such as constant predictors, can achieve high agreement. This is analogous to how accuracy can be misleading for imbalanced classification problems. In classification, this is solved by focusing on balanced accuracy instead. By analogy, we introduce the balanced agreement of s ˜ with s as q ( m ) + p ( m ) BA( m ) := .

The next theorem confirms that controlling BA( m ) yields meaningful lower bounds on the squared correlation. Theorem 12. For any value of BA( m ) , we have: 4 b ( m )(1 − b ( m ))(2BA( m ) − 1) 2 ≤ ρ ( s ( m ) , s ˜ ( m )) 2 ≤ | 2BA( m ) − 1 | .

Theorem 12 is proven in Appendix C.4. It also provides another upper bound on sample efficiency gains from access to s ˜ : Whenever BA( m ) is close to half, access to s ˜ can not improve sample efficiency by much and the sample efficiency factor τ = 1 is close to one. As a corollary, we max 1 − ρ 2 show that whenever the proxy has reasonable balanced accuracy, the minimum of the true positive rate p and true negative rate q upper bounds the squared correlation ρ 2 : Corollary 13. Whenever BA( m ) ≥ 0 . 5 , we have ρ ( s ( m ) , s ˜ ( m )) 2 ≤ min { p ( m ) ,q ( m ) } .

Proof. Given BA( m ) ≥ 0 . 5 , | 2BA( m ) − 1 | is maximized at the maximal value of BA( m ) . But with min { p ( m ) ,q ( m ) } ≤ r , that is at r +1 . Inserting into Theorem 12 yields the result.

Figure 5 shows actual value of the maximal sample efficiency and its lower and upper bounds on τ derived from Theorem 12 for a variety of models. In line with our theoretical results, τ max max lies consistently between the lower and upper bounds. Interestingly, the lower bound appear to be considerably tighter than the upper bound, that is, τ is much closer to the lower bound than the max upper bound throughout the experiments.

