Figure 2: Method overview of DiffILO. We first transform the primal discrete and constrained problem into a continuous, unconstrained, and differentiable (a.e.) problem, as depicted by the blue arrows. DiffILO employs a graph neural network (GNN) to predict solutions, as depicted by the black arrows. It optimizes both the ILP problem and the GNN parameters simultaneously through gradient descent, as depicted by the red arrows.', 396, 1682, 'high')
ON CONFLICT DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO paper_text_chunks (chunk_id, paper_version_id, section_id, sequence_index, chunk_index_in_section, page_start, page_end, content_type, text_content, token_count, char_count, location_confidence)
VALUES ('paper_049_iclr_2025_proceedings_chunk_0008', 'paper_049_iclr_2025_proceedings', 'paper_049_iclr_2025_proceedings_sec_3_1', 8, 0, 4, 5, 'body_text', 'We focus on integer linear programs (ILPs) that take the form of: min (cid:8) c ⊤ x Ax b , x 0 , 1 n (cid:9) . (P1) x | ≤ ∈ { } where n denotes the number of variables, c R n denotes the objective coefficients, the matrix A = ( a , a , , a ) ⊤ R m × n denotes the ∈ constraint coefficient matrix, m denotes the number 1 2 m of constraints, ··· and b R m ∈ denotes the right-hand-side biases of the constraints. ∈ Remark 1. Without loss of generality, we focus on ILPs with binary integer variables, also referred to as 0-1 programs. This simplification is reasonable given the fact that any bounded integer program can be converted into a binary (0-1) form (Dantzig, 2016; Ye et al., 2023a).

An ILP is quintessentially a discrete and constrained optimization problem. Our first step is to reformulate it into a continuous problem. An intuitive approach is to relax the binary variables x 0 , 1 n into continuous variables x ˆ [0 , 1] n , which is commonly known as the linear pro- ∈ { } ∈ gramming (LP) relaxation (Agmon, 1954; Bixby et al., 2004). However, clearly such relaxation alters the original solution space. To achieve an equivalent reformulation, we adopt a probabilistic approach (Karalias & Loukas, 2020), interpreting the continuous variables x ˆ as probabilities associated with the binary variables. Specifically, each binary variable x is assumed to follow a Bernoulli i distribution, writing x Bernoulli(ˆ x ) . The distribution of x is denoted as x p ( x ˆ ) . Based on i i ∼ ∼ ·| the probabilistic modeling, we reformulate the primal problem (P1) into: min (cid:8) c ⊤ x ˆ E [max( Ax b , 0 )] = 0 , x ˆ [0 , 1] n (cid:9) . (P2) x ∼ p ( ·| x ˆ ) x ˆ | − ∈

Remark 2. The novel reformulation in (P2) is motivated by the intuition that minimizing the expected constraint violations can restrict the distribution’s support to the set of optimal solutions. For example, if a component x ˆ (0 , 1) , then the constraint E [max( ax b, 0)] = 0 i x ∼ Bernoulli(ˆ x i ) ∈ − will ensure that ax b holds for both x = 0 and x = 1 . Otherwise, if x ˆ = 0 or x ˆ = 1 , i i ≤ Bernoulli(ˆ x ) becomes a deterministic distribution and it indicates that a x ˆ b holds. i i ≤ We now present the fundamental theoretical properties of (P2), establishing its equivalence with the primal problem (P1). Theorem 1 demonstrates the equivalence betweeen the two problems in terms of feasibility and solvability. Then, Theorem 2 shows that, loosely speaking, the optimal solutions to (P2) coincide with the optimal solutions to (P1). Theorem 1. The problem (P2) is feasible (and solvable, i.e., it admits at least one optimal solution) if and only if (P1) is feasible (and solvable).

Theorem 2. Let ≜ i [ n ] : c = 0 . Then the following statements hold: c i I { ∈ ̸ } 1. Suppose x ∗ 0 , 1 n is an optimal solution to (P1). Then x ∗ is also an optimal solution to (P2). If a vector x ˆ ∈ ∗ { [0 , } 1] n is a feasible solution to (P2) and satisfies x ˆ ∗ = x ∗ for all i , then x ˆ ∗ ∈ i i ∈ I c is an optimal solution to (P2). 2. Suppose x ˆ ∗ [0 , 1] n is an optimal solution to (P2). Then we have x ˆ ∗ 0 , 1 for all i . i c ∈ ∗ ∗ n ∈ ∗ { ∗ } ∈ I Let ∗ = i [ n ] : x ˆ 0 , 1 . If a vector x 0 , 1 satisfies x = x ˆ for all i ∗ , x ˆ i i i x ˆ I ∗ { ∈ ∈ { }} ∈ { } ∈ I then x is an optimal solution to (P1).', 924, 3302, 'high')
