INSERT INTO paper_text_chunks (chunk_id, paper_version_id, section_id, sequence_index, chunk_index_in_section, page_start, page_end, content_type, text_content, token_count, char_count, location_confidence)
VALUES ('paper_049_iclr_2025_proceedings_chunk_0012', 'paper_049_iclr_2025_proceedings', 'paper_049_iclr_2025_proceedings_sec_3_3', 12, 0, 6, 7, 'body_text', 'So far, we have transformed the original ILP problem into a continuous, unconstrained, and differentiable (a.e.) problem that can be efficiently solved using straightforward gradient descent. Building on this transformation, we introduce the model implementation of DiffILO, which simultaneously optimizes both the problem and the predictor parameters through direct gradient back-propagation, eliminating the need for labeled data. In line with the established practices in the field (Gasse et al., 2019; Han et al., 2023), we represent each ILP instance—which is formulated as (P1)—as a bipartite

graph = ( , ) , where and denote the sets of variables and constraints, respectively, G V ∪ W E V W and denotes the edges corresponding to the coefficients. Further details on the data representation E are available in Appendix B.1. The predictor f , which is parameterized by θ and assumed to be θ differentiable with respect to θ , outputs the predicted variable probabilities x ˆ = f ( ) . The model θ G architecture is implemented as a Graph Neural Network (GNN) (Kipf & Welling, 2017; Shi et al., 2023; 2024; 2025), followed by a multilayer perceptron (MLP). The final layer applies a sigmoid function to ensure that the output probabilities x ˆ (0 , 1) n . More details on the model architecture ∈ are provided in Appendix B.2.

Model Training During training, we update the parameters θ by optimizing the merit function ( f ( )) , as defined in (P4), through gradient descent. Specifically, let = , , µ θ 1 |D| F G D {G ··· G } be a batch of ILP instances, each represented as a bipartite graph , with n variables and m i i i G constraints. Let φ ( ) be the penalty function—corresponding to the previously defined φ ( ) —for i,j j · · the i th instance , i.e., i G φ (ˆ x ; ϵ ) ≜ (cid:0) a ⊤ ξ (ˆ x ; ϵ ) b (cid:1) I (cid:0) a ⊤ ψ (ˆ x ; ϵ ) b > 0 (cid:1) , (6) i,j i,j i,j i,j i,j − − where a and b denote the coefficients and the right-hand-side terms of the j th constraints, rei,j i,j spectively. For each instance , we sample K random vectors ϵ ( k ) (0 , 1) n i , where K is a i i G ∼ U hyperparameter. The training loss for this batch is defined as:   |D| |D| m i K 1 (cid:88) 1 (cid:88) (cid:88) (cid:88) ( k ) ( θ ; ) ≜ ( θ ; ) =  c ⊤ f ( ) + µ φ ( f ( ); ϵ )  . (7) i θ i i,j θ i i L D L G G G |D| i =1 |D| i =1 j =1 k =1 The gradient of the loss function is then given by ( θ ; ) θ ∇ L D     1 (cid:88) |D|   (cid:88) (cid:18) ∂ (cid:16) (cid:17)(cid:19)   =  f ( ) ⊤  c + µ a ξ f ( ); ϵ ( k )   .  θ θ i  i,j ∂ x ˆ θ i i   ∇ G ⊙ ⊙ G     |D| i =1 1 ≤ j ≤ m i , 1 ≤ k ≤ K : a ⊤ ψ ( f θ ( G i ); ϵ ( k ) ) − b j > 0 i,j i (8) We stabilize the training process through three useful techniques. First, to accommodate instances with ranging sizes and coefficient ranges, we apply a normalization to modify the loss function. Second, we find cosine annealing (Loshchilov & Hutter, 2016) to be beneficial for optimizing the learning schedule. Third, since the penalty coefficient µ is a critical hyperparameter in training, we introduce a dynamic and adaptive method for adjusting µ . These training techniques are further explained in detail in Appendix C.2.', 879, 3195, 'high')
