INSERT INTO paper_text_chunks (chunk_id, paper_version_id, section_id, sequence_index, chunk_index_in_section, page_start, page_end, content_type, text_content, token_count, char_count, location_confidence)
VALUES ('paper_049_iclr_2025_proceedings_chunk_0014', 'paper_049_iclr_2025_proceedings', 'paper_049_iclr_2025_proceedings_sec_4', 14, 0, 8, 9, 'body_text', 'This section presents empirical results to demonstrate the effectiveness of our proposed DiffILO in (1) generating feasible and high-quality heuristic solutions in an end-to-end manner, and (2) improving the overall performance of traditional solvers to find high-quality solutions within a constrained time frame. We then conduct a case study to provide some additional insights into the optimization process of DiffILO. All training and evaluations are performed using the same hardware configuration, specifically an Intel(R) Xeon(R) Gold 6246R CPU @ 3.40GHz, and an NVIDIA GeForce RTX 3090 GPU. Code is available at https://github.com/MIRALab-USTC/L2O-DiffILO . More experimental details can be found in Appendix C.

Benchmarks We evaluate our method mainly on three widely used ILP problem benchmarks: set covering (SC) (Balas & Ho, 1980), maximum independent set prob- 300 PS(datacollection) PS(training) lem (IS) (Bergman et al., 2015), and combinatorial auctions DiffILO(training) (CA) (Leyton-Brown et al., 2000). The datasets are generated using the code from Gasse et al. (2019). Following the settings ) h 200 ( described in the PS paper (Han et al., 2023), we generate 400 e m i 150 T instances for each benchmark, 240 for training, 60 for valida- 100 tion, and 100 for testing, respectively. Additional details about these datasets can be found in Appendix C.1. To demonstrate the effectiveness of DiffILO on realistic datasets, we conduct 0 SC IS CA experiments on two subsets of MIPLIB 2017 (Gleixner et al., 2021). More details are in Appendix D.3. Figure3: TrainingtimesofPSand DiffILOondifferentdatasets. Data Baselines We compare our method against two main cat- collection refers to the time spent egories of baselines. First, we include traditional solvers, on solving training and validation SCIP (Achterberg, 2009) and Gurobi (Gurobi Optimization, instances to obtain labels, while 2021), to evaluate whether the heuristic solutions generated training denotes the time spent on by DiffILO can accelerate the solving process. Second, we training the neural networks. compare DiffILO with the Predict-and-Search (PS) framework (Han et al., 2023), which first predicts solutions and then employs SCIP or Gurobi to search withinatrustregionforfurtherimprovement. PSandNeuralDiving(ND)(Nairetal.,2020)areboth representative supervised learning methods. However, since the prediction components in PS and ND arethe same, we primarily includePS asour baseline. Somemore recentsupervised learning approaches such as those based on contrastive learning (Huang et al., 2024) or diffusion models (Zeng et al., 2024) have not been implemented. Although these methods can enhance the performance of supervised learning, they also lead to additional training time. For fairness, DiffILO does not employ similar additional tricks either. The comparison includes capabilities of both methods to generate feasible solutions and to improve the performance of Gurobi and SCIP. We provide the results of some additional baselines, including ablation studies, in Appendix D.2.

Table 1: Average objective values obtained by different approaches at 10 , 100 , and 1 , 000 seconds. We mark the best values in bold and underline the second-best values. Best known solution (BKS) refers to the solution obtained by running Gurobi for 3,600 seconds. SC (min, BKS: 86.45) IS (max, BKS:684.14) CA (max, BKS:22272.55) 10s 100s 1000s 10s 100s 1000s 10s 100s 1000s', 660, 3457, 'high')
