INSERT INTO paper_text_chunks (chunk_id, paper_version_id, section_id, sequence_index, chunk_index_in_section, page_start, page_end, content_type, text_content, token_count, char_count, location_confidence)
VALUES ('paper_049_iclr_2025_proceedings_chunk_0028', 'paper_049_iclr_2025_proceedings', 'paper_049_iclr_2025_proceedings_sec_b_1', 31, 0, 19, 19, 'body_text', 'Following previous works (Gasse et al., 2019; Han et al., 2023; Geng et al., 2023; Wang et al., 2023b), we represent each ILP problem as a weighted bipartite graph = ( , ) , where and G V ∪W E V denote the sets of variables and constraints, respectively. The graph is equipped with a tuple of W feature matrices ( V , W , E ) , and the description of these features can be found in Table 2.

Table 2: Description of variable, constraint, and edge features in our bipartite graph representation. Tensor Feature Description Objective Normalized objective coefficient. Variable coefficient Average variable coefficient in all constraints. V Variable degree Degree of the variable node in the bipartite graph representation. Maximum variable coefficient Maximum variable coefficient in all constraints. Minimum variable coefficient Minimum variable coefficient in all constraints. Constraint coefficient Average of all coefficients in the constraint. W Constraint degree Degree of constraint nodes. Bias Normalized right-hand-side of the constraint. E Coefficient Constraint coefficient.', 216, 1083, 'high')
ON CONFLICT DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO paper_text_chunks (chunk_id, paper_version_id, section_id, sequence_index, chunk_index_in_section, page_start, page_end, content_type, text_content, token_count, char_count, location_confidence)
VALUES ('paper_049_iclr_2025_proceedings_chunk_0029', 'paper_049_iclr_2025_proceedings', 'paper_049_iclr_2025_proceedings_sec_b_2', 32, 0, 19, 19, 'body_text', 'We employ a graph neural network (GNN), parameterized by θ , as the predictor. Specifically, given a bipartite graph = ( , ) equipped with the feature metrices ( V , W , E ) , we use MLPs as G V ∪ W E embedding layers to obtain the initial embeddings : h (0) = MLP ( v ) , h (0) = MLP ( w ) , h = MLP ( e ) . (22) v θ i w θ j e ij θ ij i j

After that, we perform K graph convolution layers, with each layer in the form of two interleaved half-convolutions (Gasse et al., 2019), defined as follows:   (cid:88) (cid:16) (cid:17) h ( k +1) MLP h ( k ) , MLP h ( k ) , h , h ( k ) , w θ  w ϕ w e ij v  i ← i i j j : e ij ∈E   (cid:88) (cid:16) (cid:17) h ( k +1) MLP  h ( k ) , MLP h ( k +1) , h , h ( k )  . (23) v j ← ϕ v j ϕ w i e ij v j i : e ij ∈E

Each convolution layer is followed by two GraphNorm layers, one for variables and the other for constraints. We employ a concatenation Jumping Knowledge layer to aggregate information from all K layers and obtain the final node representations: (cid:18) (cid:16) (cid:17)(cid:19) (cid:18) (cid:16) (cid:17)(cid:19) h = MLP CONCAT h ( k ) , h = MLP CONCAT h ( k ) . (24) v i θ v w j θ w k =0 , ··· ,K i k =0 , ··· ,K j

Subsequently, we use another MLP to output the predicted logits for each variable:

z = MLP ( h ) . (25) v i θ v i

The logits are then used for resampling module, followed by a sigmoid function to output the generated solutions.', 438, 1407, 'high')
